-5d39-ab4e-db60fd086832" :question/math-academy-id "q-141264" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $4$ and $5$, respectively, what is the degree of $P+Q?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $4,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{4}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $5,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{5}.$\n\nIn $P+Q,$ the highest-order term still contains an ${x}^{5}.$ No ${x}^{5}$ term in $P$ could possibly cancel out the ${x}^{5}$ term in $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must be $5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-97fda8ea-7946-555e-8e3d-7c1f8f5cb679"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-97fda8ea-7946-555e-8e3d-7c1f8f5cb679" :answer-field/id #uuid "97fda8ea-7946-555e-8e3d-7c1f8f5cb679" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3e0da817-2175-5afa-ae23-a7f075ad5147" :answer-field/choices ["answer-04cfb9d2-2cd0-5712-b30e-ce6cf00a4c92" "answer-bbe0bbef-b70c-5b78-9300-508da745c3b8" "answer-2348e195-30db-5d7f-a0e6-010a69c22521" "answer-3e0da817-2175-5afa-ae23-a7f075ad5147" "answer-865ed96e-2f21-5df9-8914-9923d592ed7c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04cfb9d2-2cd0-5712-b30e-ce6cf00a4c92" :answer/id #uuid "04cfb9d2-2cd0-5712-b30e-ce6cf00a4c92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbe0bbef-b70c-5b78-9300-508da745c3b8" :answer/id #uuid "bbe0bbef-b70c-5b78-9300-508da745c3b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2348e195-30db-5d7f-a0e6-010a69c22521" :answer/id #uuid "2348e195-30db-5d7f-a0e6-010a69c22521" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e0da817-2175-5afa-ae23-a7f075ad5147" :answer/id #uuid "3e0da817-2175-5afa-ae23-a7f075ad5147" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-865ed96e-2f21-5df9-8914-9923d592ed7c" :answer/id #uuid "865ed96e-2f21-5df9-8914-9923d592ed7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-78e65e3d-79b6-5ea5-b7c8-3423e62535bc" :question/id #uuid "78e65e3d-79b6-5ea5-b7c8-3423e62535bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-269924" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $11$ and $10$, respectively, the degree of $P+Q$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $11,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{11}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $10,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{10}.$\n\nIn $P+Q,$ the highest-order term still contains an ${x}^{11}.$ No ${x}^{11}$ term in $P$ could possibly cancel out the ${x}^{11}$ term in $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must be $11.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fd91f76b-a516-5ea2-bf7f-fa1bf44a8403"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fd91f76b-a516-5ea2-bf7f-fa1bf44a8403" :answer-field/id #uuid "fd91f76b-a516-5ea2-bf7f-fa1bf44a8403" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-88292a8a-7973-5f2a-9503-7a6937881e83" :answer-field/choices ["answer-88292a8a-7973-5f2a-9503-7a6937881e83"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88292a8a-7973-5f2a-9503-7a6937881e83" :answer/id #uuid "88292a8a-7973-5f2a-9503-7a6937881e83" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-617b4238-abb7-5b3b-bc81-af12e3c18aa5" :question/id #uuid "617b4238-abb7-5b3b-bc81-af12e3c18aa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-51541" :question/problem "Given that $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $4,$ which of the following statements are always true?\n\nI. $\\frac{{x}^{4}}{P(x)}$ is a polynomial\n\nII. ${x}^{4}\\cdot P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $8$\n\nIII. ${x}^{4}+P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{4}+2.$ Then $\\frac{{x}^{4}}{P(x)}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}+2},$ which is not a polynomial.\n\n- Statement II is true. The highest-degree term in $P$ must contain ${x}^{4}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in ${x}^{4}\\cdot P(x)$ must contain ${x}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{4}={x}^{4+4}={x}^{8},$ meaning that ${x}^{4}\\cdot P(x)$ has degree $8.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P=-{x}^{4}.$ Then ${x}^{4}+P(x)={x}^{4}-{x}^{4}=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $4.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6c813721-2908-5a92-be17-13c7cb6f3034"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6c813721-2908-5a92-be17-13c7cb6f3034" :answer-field/id #uuid "6c813721-2908-5a92-be17-13c7cb6f3034" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c7ce5e7a-fe12-56de-9ac6-5f857ea15a0a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5175a5b8-1b67-52c5-bb4f-d2031c4d90f9" "answer-e3905f1a-6f12-51e1-81b5-1c33222125ec" "answer-246e2940-5476-5f15-bd1c-9844fcb51470" "answer-c7ce5e7a-fe12-56de-9ac6-5f857ea15a0a" "answer-b3d95f9f-44bf-5ba3-ac57-4899559d619a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5175a5b8-1b67-52c5-bb4f-d2031c4d90f9" :answer/id #uuid "5175a5b8-1b67-52c5-bb4f-d2031c4d90f9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3905f1a-6f12-51e1-81b5-1c33222125ec" :answer/id #uuid "e3905f1a-6f12-51e1-81b5-1c33222125ec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-246e2940-5476-5f15-bd1c-9844fcb51470" :answer/id #uuid "246e2940-5476-5f15-bd1c-9844fcb51470" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7ce5e7a-fe12-56de-9ac6-5f857ea15a0a" :answer/id #uuid "c7ce5e7a-fe12-56de-9ac6-5f857ea15a0a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3d95f9f-44bf-5ba3-ac57-4899559d619a" :answer/id #uuid "b3d95f9f-44bf-5ba3-ac57-4899559d619a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-40dff619-4373-5ec6-a963-8c0ddd65024c" :question/id #uuid "40dff619-4373-5ec6-a963-8c0ddd65024c" :question/math-academy-id "q-111387" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $7,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P+Q$ is a polynomial of degree $3$\n\nII. $P-Q$ is always a polynomial\n\nIII. $\\frac{2{x}^{5}\\cdot P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}$ and ${x}^{7},$ respectively. Since there is no ${x}^{7}$ term in $P$ that could possibly cancel the ${x}^{7}$ term in $Q,$ the leading term in $P+Q$ still contains ${x}^{7}.$ Therefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must always be $7.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Statement III is true. For example, suppose $P={x}^{3}$ and $Q={x}^{7}.$ Then, $\\frac{2{x}^{5}\\cdot P}{Q}=\\frac{2{x}^{5}\\cdot {x}^{3}}{{x}^{7}}=2x,$ which is a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-317c380e-b8ef-5ea3-b2f2-20c14b4d4859"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-317c380e-b8ef-5ea3-b2f2-20c14b4d4859" :answer-field/id #uuid "317c380e-b8ef-5ea3-b2f2-20c14b4d4859" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-82a41674-bf89-54e4-b672-208eafd52df6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b2492f6d-535d-5780-9d80-a92596e9f6a4" "answer-0b628a86-1b5b-5261-81fe-b8ec5b9b6d66" "answer-82a41674-bf89-54e4-b672-208eafd52df6" "answer-59b6ab9a-09a2-534d-bb1d-5e67fdedfdd7" "answer-013b2dbc-18f3-55e3-9565-85cf066921b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2492f6d-535d-5780-9d80-a92596e9f6a4" :answer/id #uuid "b2492f6d-535d-5780-9d80-a92596e9f6a4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b628a86-1b5b-5261-81fe-b8ec5b9b6d66" :answer/id #uuid "0b628a86-1b5b-5261-81fe-b8ec5b9b6d66" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82a41674-bf89-54e4-b672-208eafd52df6" :answer/id #uuid "82a41674-bf89-54e4-b672-208eafd52df6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59b6ab9a-09a2-534d-bb1d-5e67fdedfdd7" :answer/id #uuid "59b6ab9a-09a2-534d-bb1d-5e67fdedfdd7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-013b2dbc-18f3-55e3-9565-85cf066921b7" :answer/id #uuid "013b2dbc-18f3-55e3-9565-85cf066921b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124505 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cb8dda76-cb23-5b23-b067-bd505ebd9a72"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-cb8dda76-cb23-5b23-b067-bd505ebd9a72" :question/id #uuid "cb8dda76-cb23-5b23-b067-bd505ebd9a72" :question/math-academy-id "q-73861" :question/problem "Given the hyperbolic paraboloid $(x-4)^{2}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{7}=z-12$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $x=0$ and $z=0$ in the equation of the paraboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{7} & =0-12 \\\\ 16-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{7} & =-12 \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{7} & =28 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =196 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm 14 \\\\ y & =-1\\pm 14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $y=-15$ and $y=13.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,-15,0)$ and $(0,13,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3925c411-60a1-5817-a407-541f7c2a0413"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3925c411-60a1-5817-a407-541f7c2a0413" :answer-field/id #uuid "3925c411-60a1-5817-a407-541f7c2a0413" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-daa884be-dd4f-502c-ad7e-8f18e4feeeb2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-daa884be-dd4f-502c-ad7e-8f18e4feeeb2" "answer-ea74f780-c5a4-51a5-8a9e-88c3509c0e76" "answer-df704459-6416-5914-ac68-faa71d081a15" "answer-27aec271-8375-514d-93f8-b0fd659cac22" "answer-c76196c7-5bd8-564e-ab0c-1aa19bb1b301"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-daa884be-dd4f-502c-ad7e-8f18e4feeeb2" :answer/id #uuid "daa884be-dd4f-502c-ad7e-8f18e4feeeb2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,-15,0)$ and $(0,13,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea74f780-c5a4-51a5-8a9e-88c3509c0e76" :answer/id #uuid "ea74f780-c5a4-51a5-8a9e-88c3509c0e76" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,-15,0)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df704459-6416-5914-ac68-faa71d081a15" :answer/id #uuid "df704459-6416-5914-ac68-faa71d081a15" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,1,0)$ and $(0,3,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27aec271-8375-514d-93f8-b0fd659cac22" :answer/id #uuid "27aec271-8375-514d-93f8-b0fd659cac22" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,1,0)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c76196c7-5bd8-564e-ab0c-1aa19bb1b301" :answer/id #uuid "c76196c7-5bd8-564e-ab0c-1aa19bb1b301" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,13,0)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-391423b8-8e38-5e8d-89bc-42780e5b8cc3" :activity/id #uuid "391423b8-8e38-5e8d-89bc-42780e5b8cc3" :activity/title "Paraboloids" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048510 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-c7546549-9970-5f28-9b36-0561773b63db" "step-dc262a63-5e8d-5514-8ffc-08ac56ed1b39" "step-2c17976b-4b0a-57a1-bc65-e6a517f58326" "step-170e1b48-2597-50cf-8dee-08d57238b8da" "step-6e69c83b-f889-52ac-b9ce-28e3b44e9656"] :activity/first-step "step-c7546549-9970-5f28-9b36-0561773b63db" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-c7546549-9970-5f28-9b36-0561773b63db" :step/id #uuid "c7546549-9970-5f28-9b36-0561773b63db" :step/content 17592186381910 :step/next "step-dc262a63-5e8d-5514-8ffc-08ac56ed1b39" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-dc262a63-5e8d-5514-8ffc-08ac56ed1b39" :step/id #uuid "dc262a63-5e8d-5514-8ffc-08ac56ed1b39" :step/content "question-cb8dda76-cb23-5b23-b067-bd505ebd9a72" :step/next "step-2c17976b-4b0a-57a1-bc65-e6a517f58326" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2c17976b-4b0a-57a1-bc65-e6a517f58326" :step/id #uuid "2c17976b-4b0a-57a1-bc65-e6a517f58326" :step/content 17592186342099 :step/next "step-170e1b48-2597-50cf-8dee-08d57238b8da" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-170e1b48-2597-50cf-8dee-08d57238b8da" :step/id #uuid "170e1b48-2597-50cf-8dee-08d57238b8da" :step/content 17592186284461 :step/next "step-6e69c83b-f889-52ac-b9ce-28e3b44e9656" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6e69c83b-f889-52ac-b9ce-28e3b44e9656" :step/id #uuid "6e69c83b-f889-52ac-b9ce-28e3b44e9656" :step/content 17592186284465 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162009 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-aa5d7a51-2b25-530e-bc34-f2b29d7db7d9" "question-95072baf-8f6f-5ac9-b278-bac347e84d65"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162636 :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are negative semi-definite?\n\nI. ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3$\n\nIII. ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is *not* negative semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ is positive).\n\n- Quadratic form II is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are non-positive.\n\n- Quadratic form III is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are non-positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161108 :answer-field/correct 17592186161113 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-aa5d7a51-2b25-530e-bc34-f2b29d7db7d9" :question/id #uuid "aa5d7a51-2b25-530e-bc34-f2b29d7db7d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94788" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{3}$ are negative-definite?\n\nI. ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-9$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-5$\n\nIII. ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3$" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn. \n\n- Quadratic form I is negative-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are negative.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* negative-definite since not all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are negative (for example, ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$ is not negative).\n\n- Quadratic form III is *not* negative-definite since not all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are negative (${\\lambda }_{2}=10$ is not negative).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-feddc0fa-b88d-5ca9-bd43-99d017ae0768"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-feddc0fa-b88d-5ca9-bd43-99d017ae0768" :answer-field/id #uuid "feddc0fa-b88d-5ca9-bd43-99d017ae0768" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-12d6028b-8c3e-5aba-826a-b147b99e2f9c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2274a1ba-cb09-551d-a264-6a5d4c0e37fd" "answer-2dffc13b-109f-56f9-902b-d52e5de34dcf" "answer-12d6028b-8c3e-5aba-826a-b147b99e2f9c" "answer-1b4abab5-df3e-5347-99fb-84fa6de03a5e" "answer-bca55a76-d282-5f41-9c77-abe46819afe7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2274a1ba-cb09-551d-a264-6a5d4c0e37fd" :answer/id #uuid "2274a1ba-cb09-551d-a264-6a5d4c0e37fd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2dffc13b-109f-56f9-902b-d52e5de34dcf" :answer/id #uuid "2dffc13b-109f-56f9-902b-d52e5de34dcf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12d6028b-8c3e-5aba-826a-b147b99e2f9c" :answer/id #uuid "12d6028b-8c3e-5aba-826a-b147b99e2f9c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b4abab5-df3e-5347-99fb-84fa6de03a5e" :answer/id #uuid "1b4abab5-df3e-5347-99fb-84fa6de03a5e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bca55a76-d282-5f41-9c77-abe46819afe7" :answer/id #uuid "bca55a76-d282-5f41-9c77-abe46819afe7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-95072baf-8f6f-5ac9-b278-bac347e84d65" :question/id #uuid "95072baf-8f6f-5ac9-b278-bac347e84d65" :question/math-academy-id "q-94796" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-5-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )-1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +24 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-6,\\,-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fc3a16ca-ab9d-5910-8403-1994cf7ec17a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fc3a16ca-ab9d-5910-8403-1994cf7ec17a" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc3a16ca-ab9d-5910-8403-1994cf7ec17a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-34e801a5-3875-5ecd-b05f-15545a5c3b9f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a5a717d0-ad06-5979-9d2e-d3fb8cedc6ac" "answer-4c8fe2d0-662b-51dd-836f-b0095a0df130" "answer-df3ce743-bd9f-55c4-aa50-daa5ef48006d" "answer-34e801a5-3875-5ecd-b05f-15545a5c3b9f" "answer-342fba80-10c2-55b1-8abf-7ff9c9e264ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5a717d0-ad06-5979-9d2e-d3fb8cedc6ac" :answer/id #uuid "a5a717d0-ad06-5979-9d2e-d3fb8cedc6ac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c8fe2d0-662b-51dd-836f-b0095a0df130" :answer/id #uuid "4c8fe2d0-662b-51dd-836f-b0095a0df130" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df3ce743-bd9f-55c4-aa50-daa5ef48006d" :answer/id #uuid "df3ce743-bd9f-55c4-aa50-daa5ef48006d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34e801a5-3875-5ecd-b05f-15545a5c3b9f" :answer/id #uuid "34e801a5-3875-5ecd-b05f-15545a5c3b9f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-342fba80-10c2-55b1-8abf-7ff9c9e264ed" :answer/id #uuid "342fba80-10c2-55b1-8abf-7ff9c9e264ed" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162010 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7aa930b4-3c77-5008-aaf5-9bbf298703c7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7aa930b4-3c77-5008-aaf5-9bbf298703c7" :question/id #uuid "7aa930b4-3c77-5008-aaf5-9bbf298703c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-94800" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-7$ and $det(A)=-3$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-7$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-7<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|=3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-079db36a-f121-5453-be8e-d40d62051237"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-079db36a-f121-5453-be8e-d40d62051237" :answer-field/id #uuid "079db36a-f121-5453-be8e-d40d62051237" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f936acee-b9ba-5a2d-aab8-0d02b6a64ffc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a1ff6a24-e096-5a38-adf3-8f6baa63dc90" "answer-3543723c-355b-52c7-b10b-104509d59ebd" "answer-1c770309-60dd-57d9-8388-da614d588229" "answer-f936acee-b9ba-5a2d-aab8-0d02b6a64ffc" "answer-5585210e-e5b7-562b-96ab-a025b6173eec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1ff6a24-e096-5a38-adf3-8f6baa63dc90" :answer/id #uuid "a1ff6a24-e096-5a38-adf3-8f6baa63dc90" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3543723c-355b-52c7-b10b-104509d59ebd" :answer/id #uuid "3543723c-355b-52c7-b10b-104509d59ebd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c770309-60dd-57d9-8388-da614d588229" :answer/id #uuid "1c770309-60dd-57d9-8388-da614d588229" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f936acee-b9ba-5a2d-aab8-0d02b6a64ffc" :answer/id #uuid "f936acee-b9ba-5a2d-aab8-0d02b6a64ffc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5585210e-e5b7-562b-96ab-a025b6173eec" :answer/id #uuid "5585210e-e5b7-562b-96ab-a025b6173eec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-5dca4ae1-f844-539e-b380-ba8dbc5f5355" :activity/id #uuid "5dca4ae1-f844-539e-b380-ba8dbc5f5355" :activity/title "Positive-Definite and Negative-Definite Quadratic Forms" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048555 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-a02e4cde-95d3-5443-90f2-5b20307605ab" "step-561ae071-c31b-5dd5-8e25-204838ce97f8" "step-06a5f906-8b72-514b-9062-d1bf33299610" "step-5937adbb-f9b5-52b1-9c19-002f11b099e9" "step-014ca587-44a7-5c4b-a2b6-7b58d26e96d2"] :activity/first-step "step-a02e4cde-95d3-5443-90f2-5b20307605ab" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-a02e4cde-95d3-5443-90f2-5b20307605ab" :step/id #uuid "a02e4cde-95d3-5443-90f2-5b20307605ab" :step/content 17592186162636 :step/next "step-561ae071-c31b-5dd5-8e25-204838ce97f8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-561ae071-c31b-5dd5-8e25-204838ce97f8" :step/id #uuid "561ae071-c31b-5dd5-8e25-204838ce97f8" :step/content 17592186162638 :step/next "step-06a5f906-8b72-514b-9062-d1bf33299610" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-06a5f906-8b72-514b-9062-d1bf33299610" :step/id #uuid "06a5f906-8b72-514b-9062-d1bf33299610" :step/content "question-aa5d7a51-2b25-530e-bc34-f2b29d7db7d9" :step/next "step-5937adbb-f9b5-52b1-9c19-002f11b099e9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5937adbb-f9b5-52b1-9c19-002f11b099e9" :step/id #uuid "5937adbb-f9b5-52b1-9c19-002f11b099e9" :step/content "question-95072baf-8f6f-5ac9-b278-bac347e84d65" :step/next "step-014ca587-44a7-5c4b-a2b6-7b58d26e96d2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-014ca587-44a7-5c4b-a2b6-7b58d26e96d2" :step/id #uuid "014ca587-44a7-5c4b-a2b6-7b58d26e96d2" :step/content "question-7aa930b4-3c77-5008-aaf5-9bbf298703c7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186089249 :answer-field/choices 17592186089250] [:db/retract 17592186089249 :answer-field/choices 17592186089251] [:db/retract 17592186089249 :answer-field/choices 17592186089252] [:db/retract 17592186089249 :answer-field/choices 17592186089253] [:db/retract 17592186089249 :answer-field/choices 17592186089254] {:db/id 17592186092263 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7798a8be-b1f8-549d-9dbb-a2ddea411006"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092261 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-70b1c8a4-0636-5508-ab80-4e4e15ec81e5" "question-77c551c0-c155-56eb-9f11-d8d660941796"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092118 :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = 3\\cdot 2^u, \\qquad u=1-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 3\\cdot 2^u\\ln(2),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092513 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={e}^{2x+5},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u=2x+5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{e}^{u} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186089249 :answer-field/correct "answer-ac9861ac-ab1f-5a28-b691-91302c31566a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fc809e37-d2bf-588c-ad7b-ff6395a75916" "answer-8de0ee3e-9434-591e-863f-19dc03481eb0" "answer-ac9861ac-ab1f-5a28-b691-91302c31566a" "answer-cafdcf0f-5e0a-5f8f-978e-3e3f2f8dc403" "answer-4726f486-296b-54df-b7de-616f90a2a430"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc809e37-d2bf-588c-ad7b-ff6395a75916" :answer/id #uuid "fc809e37-d2bf-588c-ad7b-ff6395a75916" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2x+5){e}^{2x+5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8de0ee3e-9434-591e-863f-19dc03481eb0" :answer/id #uuid "8de0ee3e-9434-591e-863f-19dc03481eb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x{e}^{2x+5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac9861ac-ab1f-5a28-b691-91302c31566a" :answer/id #uuid "ac9861ac-ab1f-5a28-b691-91302c31566a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{2x+5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cafdcf0f-5e0a-5f8f-978e-3e3f2f8dc403" :answer/id #uuid "cafdcf0f-5e0a-5f8f-978e-3e3f2f8dc403" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4726f486-296b-54df-b7de-616f90a2a430" :answer/id #uuid "4726f486-296b-54df-b7de-616f90a2a430" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2x+5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186314351 :answer-field/correct "answer-f37132fc-0cae-54dc-850c-42a615eac8ec" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f37132fc-0cae-54dc-850c-42a615eac8ec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f37132fc-0cae-54dc-850c-42a615eac8ec" :answer/id #uuid "f37132fc-0cae-54dc-850c-42a615eac8ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8-3e^{-3x+5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7798a8be-b1f8-549d-9dbb-a2ddea411006" :question/id #uuid "7798a8be-b1f8-549d-9dbb-a2ddea411006" :question/math-academy-id "q-340954" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={e}^{-2{x}^{7}}$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=-2{x}^{7}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-14{x}^{6}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-14{x}^{6}) \\\\  & =-14{x}^{6}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-14{x}^{6}{e}^{-2{x}^{7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(-14{x}^{6}{e}^{-2{x}^{7}})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =-14(1)^{6}{e}^{-2(1)^{7}} \\\\  & =-14{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-11e1ae92-136f-5d68-8353-b8a4c9e485ce"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-11e1ae92-136f-5d68-8353-b8a4c9e485ce" :answer-field/id #uuid "11e1ae92-136f-5d68-8353-b8a4c9e485ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ca0e728f-63f5-5bf9-9222-2a283f84a906" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ca0e728f-63f5-5bf9-9222-2a283f84a906"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca0e728f-63f5-5bf9-9222-2a283f84a906" :answer/id #uuid "ca0e728f-63f5-5bf9-9222-2a283f84a906" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{e^2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-70b1c8a4-0636-5508-ab80-4e4e15ec81e5" :question/id #uuid "70b1c8a4-0636-5508-ab80-4e4e15ec81e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-340970" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=2\\cdot {5}^{1-3x}$ at the point where $x=0.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=2\\cdot {5}^{u},\\,u=1-3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=2\\cdot {5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {5}^{1-3x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=0$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(-6\\cdot {5}^{1-3x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {5}^{1-3(0)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =-6\\cdot 5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =-30\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d83b75c0-fe56-5390-8126-bd377ed6af9e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d83b75c0-fe56-5390-8126-bd377ed6af9e" :answer-field/id #uuid "d83b75c0-fe56-5390-8126-bd377ed6af9e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ff3851f0-019b-54f0-9d24-526c6e29abe2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ff3851f0-019b-54f0-9d24-526c6e29abe2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff3851f0-019b-54f0-9d24-526c6e29abe2" :answer/id #uuid "ff3851f0-019b-54f0-9d24-526c6e29abe2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-30\\ln(5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-77c551c0-c155-56eb-9f11-d8d660941796" :question/id #uuid "77c551c0-c155-56eb-9f11-d8d660941796" :question/math-academy-id "q-107463" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={4}^{2x+1}$ at the point where $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={4}^{u},\\,u=2x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={4}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={4}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{2x+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=-1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & ={(2\\cdot {4}^{2x+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((4)^{1/2}) \\\\  & =ln\\,(\\,\\sqrt{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x={x}_{1}=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={({4}^{2x+1})|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & ={4}^{2(-1)+1} \\\\  & ={4}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-717c0cdc-ae39-5590-87a2-a75a93baebfc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-717c0cdc-ae39-5590-87a2-a75a93baebfc" :answer-field/id #uuid "717c0cdc-ae39-5590-87a2-a75a93baebfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-207209c4-3cd7-5f9a-8f55-b6d3c323a8ae" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a77b2a43-bd2d-5fe7-a7da-2538cecd54fa" "answer-8d428376-e2b0-53e2-b314-797b7522cd0c" "answer-47a3f504-906f-5250-a397-60c7605afb97" "answer-207209c4-3cd7-5f9a-8f55-b6d3c323a8ae" "answer-e12ce1c7-49ac-5fd0-9ccc-f097ef76e6f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a77b2a43-bd2d-5fe7-a7da-2538cecd54fa" :answer/id #uuid "a77b2a43-bd2d-5fe7-a7da-2538cecd54fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d428376-e2b0-53e2-b314-797b7522cd0c" :answer/id #uuid "8d428376-e2b0-53e2-b314-797b7522cd0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47a3f504-906f-5250-a397-60c7605afb97" :answer/id #uuid "47a3f504-906f-5250-a397-60c7605afb97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-207209c4-3cd7-5f9a-8f55-b6d3c323a8ae" :answer/id #uuid "207209c4-3cd7-5f9a-8f55-b6d3c323a8ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e12ce1c7-49ac-5fd0-9ccc-f097ef76e6f1" :answer/id #uuid "e12ce1c7-49ac-5fd0-9ccc-f097ef76e6f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,x-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092399 :activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093116 :step/math-academy-id 646582 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093117 :step/math-academy-id 646583 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093120 :step/math-academy-id 646586 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093118 :step/math-academy-id 646589 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188317 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b6c49e6b-d082-5bf8-805d-249ef4b78827" "question-e3b6054b-f3bd-5aa9-aa30-6fbb1ec1ef44"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188318 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-59718fdb-33b2-584f-912f-49ef25f56ae4" "question-ba02335d-7784-52da-a978-380fc6e80144"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188319 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f5944661-3e94-5778-ad45-da56e2af2415" "question-839781ca-f17e-59bc-bcb0-dc6733a65b9d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188320 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9080bcff-95c0-5480-aa15-7e41729ae6e4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b6c49e6b-d082-5bf8-805d-249ef4b78827" :question/id #uuid "b6c49e6b-d082-5bf8-805d-249ef4b78827" :question/math-academy-id "q-144845" :question/problem "![](images/69/69ae26109021b156ae22879f30a64c393fa9a870d207be72c75533c8b6918bb9.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $2$ and $5,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,6]$ given that $f(6)=3.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,2,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =3 \\\\ f(2) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{2}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-5 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ f(1) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{1}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-(5-2) \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{3,-2,0\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2595da81-f0d2-506b-9398-dff018e8c2ec"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2595da81-f0d2-506b-9398-dff018e8c2ec" :answer-field/id #uuid "2595da81-f0d2-506b-9398-dff018e8c2ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cfc7aaea-4301-584f-bdc3-af424dfb7c9e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e6ea1700-7cfc-5ba3-a0a5-c241917d08b1" "answer-4fdfdccb-f867-5185-8ab5-657123634d9f" "answer-cfc7aaea-4301-584f-bdc3-af424dfb7c9e" "answer-c3ec6c24-2292-511b-9be5-127f37247732" "answer-ac7c51fa-cfb6-5f55-b174-77dad9d56fec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6ea1700-7cfc-5ba3-a0a5-c241917d08b1" :answer/id #uuid "e6ea1700-7cfc-5ba3-a0a5-c241917d08b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4fdfdccb-f867-5185-8ab5-657123634d9f" :answer/id #uuid "4fdfdccb-f867-5185-8ab5-657123634d9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cfc7aaea-4301-584f-bdc3-af424dfb7c9e" :answer/id #uuid "cfc7aaea-4301-584f-bdc3-af424dfb7c9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3ec6c24-2292-511b-9be5-127f37247732" :answer/id #uuid "c3ec6c24-2292-511b-9be5-127f37247732" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac7c51fa-cfb6-5f55-b174-77dad9d56fec" :answer/id #uuid "ac7c51fa-cfb6-5f55-b174-77dad9d56fec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e3b6054b-f3bd-5aa9-aa30-6fbb1ec1ef44" :question/id #uuid "e3b6054b-f3bd-5aa9-aa30-6fbb1ec1ef44" :question/math-academy-id "q-344095" :question/problem "![](images/ca/cad4c483d2678c63382c13d43e3377c0a471ff510bd6b86ea37dad3adb440d9a.png)\n\nThe function $p(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-5,3].$ The graph of ${p}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $p(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $9.$\n\nGiven that $p(3)=-4,$ what is the maximum value ${p}_{\\text{max}}$ of $p(x)$ on the interval $[-5,3]?$\n\n${p}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $p(x)$ on $[-5,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${p}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-5,3),$ and ${p}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $p$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(3) & =-4 \\\\ p(-5) & =p(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-5}{p}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =p(3)-{\\int }_{-5}^{3}{p}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4-9 \\\\  & =-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $p$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $p$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{p}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-4,-13\\}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $p$ is ${p}_{\\text{max}}=-4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $p(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7d31afb4-f8c1-5171-921e-f2991ad5424b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7d31afb4-f8c1-5171-921e-f2991ad5424b" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d31afb4-f8c1-5171-921e-f2991ad5424b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-83a91dc7-6957-5530-aee4-3045d16a9de2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-83a91dc7-6957-5530-aee4-3045d16a9de2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83a91dc7-6957-5530-aee4-3045d16a9de2" :answer/id #uuid "83a91dc7-6957-5530-aee4-3045d16a9de2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-59718fdb-33b2-584f-912f-49ef25f56ae4" :question/id #uuid "59718fdb-33b2-584f-912f-49ef25f56ae4" :question/math-academy-id "q-144867" :question/problem "![](images/89/89445981059ca7902f22c916f7241573dcfa8b70798b067896b9a30d0913d251.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $3$ and $9,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,5]$ given that $f(5)=10.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =10 \\\\ f(1) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10-9 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{-2}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10-(-3+9) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{10,1,4\\}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=1,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8c5f3fbc-3413-5fe3-a14b-7220c2d641bc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8c5f3fbc-3413-5fe3-a14b-7220c2d641bc" :answer-field/id #uuid "8c5f3fbc-3413-5fe3-a14b-7220c2d641bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6be9a124-f36b-5039-beb4-9169cea0786a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-261a9480-07c6-5439-8161-94b991801e54" "answer-6be9a124-f36b-5039-beb4-9169cea0786a" "answer-06b8fe3d-8331-5609-a33b-334910eff9b9" "answer-c8e80193-25cd-5cb0-a082-8787876542f3" "answer-2be118f0-398f-5be1-86b7-522a08c5d5da"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-261a9480-07c6-5439-8161-94b991801e54" :answer/id #uuid "261a9480-07c6-5439-8161-94b991801e54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6be9a124-f36b-5039-beb4-9169cea0786a" :answer/id #uuid "6be9a124-f36b-5039-beb4-9169cea0786a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06b8fe3d-8331-5609-a33b-334910eff9b9" :answer/id #uuid "06b8fe3d-8331-5609-a33b-334910eff9b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8e80193-25cd-5cb0-a082-8787876542f3" :answer/id #uuid "c8e80193-25cd-5cb0-a082-8787876542f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2be118f0-398f-5be1-86b7-522a08c5d5da" :answer/id #uuid "2be118f0-398f-5be1-86b7-522a08c5d5da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ba02335d-7784-52da-a978-380fc6e80144" :question/id #uuid "ba02335d-7784-52da-a978-380fc6e80144" :question/math-academy-id "q-144840" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c79d3a4de46d41da5edec58514bd7138ff3369032c76c758d57d3816f99e3ef3.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-1,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $10.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-1,6]$ given that $f(6)=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-1,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =-2 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{-1}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2-(-10) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-2,8\\}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e5d30eaa-4a91-54ae-a4e2-bfc408fea6d4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e5d30eaa-4a91-54ae-a4e2-bfc408fea6d4" :answer-field/id #uuid "e5d30eaa-4a91-54ae-a4e2-bfc408fea6d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cb14986c-8086-5ecf-ba35-3b4e666cd1f8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0077e303-78c8-5a4d-9f4e-3d35cf42cb13" "answer-9d3dcf13-c55f-5e57-ab59-e148431cea27" "answer-cb14986c-8086-5ecf-ba35-3b4e666cd1f8" "answer-9b90e5cf-272b-5399-83d6-f0c4cec499fd" "answer-98599ccb-165d-5a9f-89f7-6a0fa52e415c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0077e303-78c8-5a4d-9f4e-3d35cf42cb13" :answer/id #uuid "0077e303-78c8-5a4d-9f4e-3d35cf42cb13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d3dcf13-c55f-5e57-ab59-e148431cea27" :answer/id #uuid "9d3dcf13-c55f-5e57-ab59-e148431cea27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb14986c-8086-5ecf-ba35-3b4e666cd1f8" :answer/id #uuid "cb14986c-8086-5ecf-ba35-3b4e666cd1f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b90e5cf-272b-5399-83d6-f0c4cec499fd" :answer/id #uuid "9b90e5cf-272b-5399-83d6-f0c4cec499fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98599ccb-165d-5a9f-89f7-6a0fa52e415c" :answer/id #uuid "98599ccb-165d-5a9f-89f7-6a0fa52e415c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f5944661-3e94-5778-ad45-da56e2af2415" :question/id #uuid "f5944661-3e94-5778-ad45-da56e2af2415" :question/math-academy-id "q-344398" :question/problem "![](images/66/6613de40d3150985d471d97fd3b5369cd67df2ae248b22ce99af011f3484a20c.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-5,3].$ The graph of ${g}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $g(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $5,$ $4,$ and $7$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $g(x)$ on the interval $[-5,3]$ given that $g(-2)=3.$\n\n${g}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $g(x)$ on $[-5,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${g}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-5,3),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2,0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,-2,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $g$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-2) & =3 \\\\ g(0) & =g(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{g}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =3+4 \\\\  & =7 \\\\ g(3) & =g(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{g}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =7+(-7) \\\\  & =0 \\\\ g(-5) & =g(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-5}{g}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =g(-2)-{\\int }_{-5}^{-2}{g}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =3-5 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $g$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $g$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{3,7,0,-2\\}=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $g$ is ${g}_{\\text{max}}=7,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $g(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-36e143a5-6376-5cd6-8c7d-32d7f7188f06"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-36e143a5-6376-5cd6-8c7d-32d7f7188f06" :answer-field/id #uuid "36e143a5-6376-5cd6-8c7d-32d7f7188f06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9dcfb3be-68ac-5ef8-ac92-f7ed17e3cd85" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9dcfb3be-68ac-5ef8-ac92-f7ed17e3cd85"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9dcfb3be-68ac-5ef8-ac92-f7ed17e3cd85" :answer/id #uuid "9dcfb3be-68ac-5ef8-ac92-f7ed17e3cd85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-839781ca-f17e-59bc-bcb0-dc6733a65b9d" :question/id #uuid "839781ca-f17e-59bc-bcb0-dc6733a65b9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344414" :question/problem "![](images/24/24ee434f11bca408587e1baa6b9189e430fa745cef0668bc35cd871761f410ed.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,5].$ The graph of ${h}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $h(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $2,$ $6,$ and $4$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $h(x)$ on the interval $[-3,5]$ given that $h(3)=2.$\n\n${h}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $h(x)$ on $[-3,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${h}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,5),$ and ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1,3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,-1,3,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $h$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(3) & =2 \\\\ h(5) & =h(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{5}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =2+4 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ h(-1) & =h(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-1}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =h(3)-{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =2-(-6) \\\\  & =8 \\\\ h(-3) & =h(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{-3}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =h(-1)-{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =8-(-2) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $h$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $h$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{h}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,6,8,10\\}=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $h$ is ${h}_{\\text{max}}=10,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $h(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7ec89a11-249d-5e23-acad-e5fe2601910b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7ec89a11-249d-5e23-acad-e5fe2601910b" :answer-field/id #uuid "7ec89a11-249d-5e23-acad-e5fe2601910b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c5f0e03b-b91a-5f40-83a8-85dcfb2ab7ab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c5f0e03b-b91a-5f40-83a8-85dcfb2ab7ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5f0e03b-b91a-5f40-83a8-85dcfb2ab7ab" :answer/id #uuid "c5f0e03b-b91a-5f40-83a8-85dcfb2ab7ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9080bcff-95c0-5480-aa15-7e41729ae6e4" :question/id #uuid "9080bcff-95c0-5480-aa15-7e41729ae6e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-104260" :question/problem "![](images/39/39ee1ed9a272b610d17b52f5225a1fcbcfa9966e0eb083dd8c9d53b1cd29b8b9.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-5,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $11,$ $2$ and $2,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-5,4]$ if $f(0)=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-5,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-5,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,0,2,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =-2 \\\\ f(-5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{-5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(0)-{\\int }_{-5}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2-(-11) \\\\  & =9 \\\\ f(2) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(2-2) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-2,9,0,-2\\}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-2,$ and the values of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ are ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0,4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-891a58fe-e347-55e1-9227-fafd48e4c74a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-891a58fe-e347-55e1-9227-fafd48e4c74a" :answer-field/id #uuid "891a58fe-e347-55e1-9227-fafd48e4c74a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e5b58c4c-fba7-5228-b103-b2cb83d6e1d5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2b298866-d4d3-50ef-99c2-892e1ccbad3c" "answer-e5b58c4c-fba7-5228-b103-b2cb83d6e1d5" "answer-68878cf0-9180-5c61-9b7c-7781cf21cc65" "answer-43752373-0228-5044-b688-cb27a6eeb3f0" "answer-8970f9a2-de34-5b1a-af61-3790f8b31e22"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b298866-d4d3-50ef-99c2-892e1ccbad3c" :answer/id #uuid "2b298866-d4d3-50ef-99c2-892e1ccbad3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5b58c4c-fba7-5228-b103-b2cb83d6e1d5" :answer/id #uuid "e5b58c4c-fba7-5228-b103-b2cb83d6e1d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68878cf0-9180-5c61-9b7c-7781cf21cc65" :answer/id #uuid "68878cf0-9180-5c61-9b7c-7781cf21cc65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43752373-0228-5044-b688-cb27a6eeb3f0" :answer/id #uuid "43752373-0228-5044-b688-cb27a6eeb3f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8970f9a2-de34-5b1a-af61-3790f8b31e22" :answer/id #uuid "8970f9a2-de34-5b1a-af61-3790f8b31e22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188527 :question/problem "![](images/fe/fedd81f87921d865c375ecdbf9b83439092d138686e07753bb20ae522b6e49c9.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,3]$ given that $f(0)=56.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $2$ rectangles and $2$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/96/962bea79c8308d1d63937aa15f19492f2dd0c31f278e2957a71255dbbd92a8ca.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =56 \\\\ f(-3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{-3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(0)-{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =56-(-2-2) \\\\  & =60 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =56+2+2 \\\\  & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{56,60,60\\}=56.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=56,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186185442 :answer-field/correct 17592186185443 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176033 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-06429645-5834-5aa5-be8c-7530ab34a0e8" "question-e01898f3-3b5c-52d9-b3b4-b90e7c403824"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176341 :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(7,-2).$ So, our actual value is $y=-2.$\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression line that corresponds to $x=7$ has the $y$-coordinate $-4.$ So, our estimated value of is $\\overset{^}{y}=-4.$\n\n![](images/92/92a26b8ab019879dec3208878f6a0604c0616776b09e3f8fa916fcab72df3a73.png)\n\nTherefore, the residual is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =-2-(-4) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186174304 :answer-field/correct 17592186174308 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-06429645-5834-5aa5-be8c-7530ab34a0e8" :question/id #uuid "06429645-5834-5aa5-be8c-7530ab34a0e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-134787" :question/problem "![](images/55/553af60aa28cbdd7565259eacc167187bc9c63a5b30cd78a6c14a3c43603fca2.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for the data sets $x$ and $y$ of paired observations and the corresponding linear regression line are shown above.\n\nAccording to the regression model, what is the residual for the highlighted datapoint?" :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(5,3).$ So, our actual value is $y=3.$\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression line that corresponds to $x=5$ has the $y$-coordinate $5.$ So, our estimated value of is $\\overset{^}{y}=5.$\n\n![](images/c5/c558c80b7056b794534146d3b99f399c3084806758f78bd6eddb39e8c89248e9.png)\n\nTherefore, the residual is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =3-5 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-48948107-5211-5ac2-9e2d-522c9771683b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-48948107-5211-5ac2-9e2d-522c9771683b" :answer-field/id #uuid "48948107-5211-5ac2-9e2d-522c9771683b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e0fe8d85-7874-5929-8753-4945ee9ab9ea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-155561b0-11da-52be-a290-8bc7fd41e681" "answer-e0fe8d85-7874-5929-8753-4945ee9ab9ea" "answer-56a2ddd1-cadc-5a93-9a25-8dddff01f271" "answer-aefdfe8c-fa67-577b-98ee-259e0b602d66" "answer-e2a545ba-ef49-5db8-a0d9-0458f6933545"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-155561b0-11da-52be-a290-8bc7fd41e681" :answer/id #uuid "155561b0-11da-52be-a290-8bc7fd41e681" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0fe8d85-7874-5929-8753-4945ee9ab9ea" :answer/id #uuid "e0fe8d85-7874-5929-8753-4945ee9ab9ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56a2ddd1-cadc-5a93-9a25-8dddff01f271" :answer/id #uuid "56a2ddd1-cadc-5a93-9a25-8dddff01f271" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aefdfe8c-fa67-577b-98ee-259e0b602d66" :answer/id #uuid "aefdfe8c-fa67-577b-98ee-259e0b602d66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2a545ba-ef49-5db8-a0d9-0458f6933545" :answer/id #uuid "e2a545ba-ef49-5db8-a0d9-0458f6933545" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e01898f3-3b5c-52d9-b3b4-b90e7c403824" :question/id #uuid "e01898f3-3b5c-52d9-b3b4-b90e7c403824" :question/math-academy-id "q-134799" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae35183698308f87acb254b7141d025bc55f815c49dea54231c713997c6a22eb.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for the data sets $x$ and $y$ of paired observations and the corresponding linear regression line are shown above.\n\nAccording to the regression model, what is the residual for the highlighted datapoint?" :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(10,4).$ So, our actual value is $y=4.$\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression line that corresponds to $x=10$ has the $y$-coordinate $8.$ So, our estimated value of is $\\overset{^}{y}=8.$\n\n![](images/61/61ed1ba4b285ac0d21410ddde91c3ed3882def52cbc500867aa7184114dd8fd8.png)\n\nTherefore, the residual is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =4-8 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c15ed523-3e32-50d6-b2d3-15996b2f731b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c15ed523-3e32-50d6-b2d3-15996b2f731b" :answer-field/id #uuid "c15ed523-3e32-50d6-b2d3-15996b2f731b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-288479be-98bd-57e4-9608-f6f04980af8a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cc7640de-088c-554a-a442-df97fc11d882" "answer-288479be-98bd-57e4-9608-f6f04980af8a" "answer-ffffc3bb-9a0e-59c2-9d08-ba493528be35" "answer-a92e5308-f602-5b56-8117-08f3a63517ce" "answer-e5da0595-ab74-5f54-bd61-a8b4f9a3237a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc7640de-088c-554a-a442-df97fc11d882" :answer/id #uuid "cc7640de-088c-554a-a442-df97fc11d882" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-288479be-98bd-57e4-9608-f6f04980af8a" :answer/id #uuid "288479be-98bd-57e4-9608-f6f04980af8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffffc3bb-9a0e-59c2-9d08-ba493528be35" :answer/id #uuid "ffffc3bb-9a0e-59c2-9d08-ba493528be35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a92e5308-f602-5b56-8117-08f3a63517ce" :answer/id #uuid "a92e5308-f602-5b56-8117-08f3a63517ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5da0595-ab74-5f54-bd61-a8b4f9a3237a" :answer/id #uuid "e5da0595-ab74-5f54-bd61-a8b4f9a3237a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-1565ea4e-c392-50d1-9414-12d443b010cf" :activity/id #uuid "1565ea4e-c392-50d1-9414-12d443b010cf" :activity/title "Residuals and Residual Plots" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050269 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-674c6b2d-1d05-59d6-90ed-5b152a4fab06" "step-4f48dbfb-61be-5cd7-8c7c-7f3723f848b5" "step-5cc55bd4-2c1a-5ba9-bb47-99a6d6d6d26a"] :activity/first-step "step-674c6b2d-1d05-59d6-90ed-5b152a4fab06" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-674c6b2d-1d05-59d6-90ed-5b152a4fab06" :step/id #uuid "674c6b2d-1d05-59d6-90ed-5b152a4fab06" :step/content 17592186176341 :step/next "step-4f48dbfb-61be-5cd7-8c7c-7f3723f848b5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4f48dbfb-61be-5cd7-8c7c-7f3723f848b5" :step/id #uuid "4f48dbfb-61be-5cd7-8c7c-7f3723f848b5" :step/content "question-06429645-5834-5aa5-be8c-7530ab34a0e8" :step/next "step-5cc55bd4-2c1a-5ba9-bb47-99a6d6d6d26a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5cc55bd4-2c1a-5ba9-bb47-99a6d6d6d26a" :step/id #uuid "5cc55bd4-2c1a-5ba9-bb47-99a6d6d6d26a" :step/content "question-e01898f3-3b5c-52d9-b3b4-b90e7c403824" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186378942 :answer-field/choices 17592186378944] [:db/retract 17592186378942 :answer-field/choices 17592186378945] [:db/retract 17592186378942 :answer-field/choices 17592186378946] [:db/retract 17592186378942 :answer-field/choices 17592186378947] [:db/retract 17592186378942 :answer-field/choices 17592186378943] {:db/id 17592186124507 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-17beca86-e4d7-5c67-a86e-43d1aa96f62a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-17beca86-e4d7-5c67-a86e-43d1aa96f62a" :question/id #uuid "17beca86-e4d7-5c67-a86e-43d1aa96f62a" :question/math-academy-id "q-35256" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xy$-plane of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $xy$-plane, we substitute $z=0$ into the equation of the ellipsoid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of the ellipse with semi-axes $2$ and $\\sqrt{2},$ centered at $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ee99d141-0b3e-5394-b62b-4fd1bb248207"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ee99d141-0b3e-5394-b62b-4fd1bb248207" :answer-field/id #uuid "ee99d141-0b3e-5394-b62b-4fd1bb248207" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b85f60df-219f-5be3-aaaf-2385ba1bce5f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d676a34b-4dca-5450-bd6d-3361f79fa4f9" "answer-b85f60df-219f-5be3-aaaf-2385ba1bce5f" "answer-6614239e-1f45-5292-a065-0e178c240ecc" "answer-fa525cea-3517-526d-84b3-2444d744b1e5" "answer-e6f6a3eb-578c-5a4e-a845-6dfd36aaeb14"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d676a34b-4dca-5450-bd6d-3361f79fa4f9" :answer/id #uuid "d676a34b-4dca-5450-bd6d-3361f79fa4f9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An ellipse with semi-axes $2$ and $\\sqrt{2},$ centered at $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b85f60df-219f-5be3-aaaf-2385ba1bce5f" :answer/id #uuid "b85f60df-219f-5be3-aaaf-2385ba1bce5f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An ellipse with semi-axes $2$ and $\\sqrt{2},$ centered at $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6614239e-1f45-5292-a065-0e178c240ecc" :answer/id #uuid "6614239e-1f45-5292-a065-0e178c240ecc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An ellipse with semi-axes $4$ and $2,$ centered at $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa525cea-3517-526d-84b3-2444d744b1e5" :answer/id #uuid "fa525cea-3517-526d-84b3-2444d744b1e5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An ellipse with semi-axes $4$ and $2,$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6f6a3eb-578c-5a4e-a845-6dfd36aaeb14" :answer/id #uuid "e6f6a3eb-578c-5a4e-a845-6dfd36aaeb14" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An ellipse with semi-axes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2},$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186378941 :question/worked-solution "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z-5)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ (z-5)^{2} & =2(1-(x-1)^{2}-\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}) \\\\ z-5 & =\\pm \\sqrt{2(1-(x-1)^{2}-\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-(x-1)^{2}-\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}\\ge 0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse $(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}=1$ and its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is \n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186378942 :answer-field/correct "answer-c0ba014d-0ec2-55fe-bf40-d651669f4cc1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0542b6da-d2bf-5b22-bd04-20504f562c8e" "answer-c0ba014d-0ec2-55fe-bf40-d651669f4cc1" "answer-dfef3fad-9852-5933-acfa-b0120c7d2df8" "answer-2c483d3e-2246-508e-b8da-35510f1d8428" "answer-c6f713a9-8f47-5306-bfae-9b1382971838"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0542b6da-d2bf-5b22-bd04-20504f562c8e" :answer/id #uuid "0542b6da-d2bf-5b22-bd04-20504f562c8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}>1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0ba014d-0ec2-55fe-bf40-d651669f4cc1" :answer/id #uuid "c0ba014d-0ec2-55fe-bf40-d651669f4cc1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dfef3fad-9852-5933-acfa-b0120c7d2df8" :answer/id #uuid "dfef3fad-9852-5933-acfa-b0120c7d2df8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}<1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c483d3e-2246-508e-b8da-35510f1d8428" :answer/id #uuid "2c483d3e-2246-508e-b8da-35510f1d8428" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6f713a9-8f47-5306-bfae-9b1382971838" :answer/id #uuid "c6f713a9-8f47-5306-bfae-9b1382971838" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-97656ef7-8b3f-5b3b-a78e-b180f6afdb4b" :activity/id #uuid "97656ef7-8b3f-5b3b-a78e-b180f6afdb4b" :activity/title "Ellipsoids" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049477 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-3f47cbab-f727-53bf-9a6c-904dcbb4215a" "step-56ceaeb1-6ec7-595a-9937-934df0bd5805" "step-75d99a5b-c852-5111-a02c-67dea933b6f4" "step-05f9a82f-656b-50bc-8f0f-e8191e8f5a42" "step-8beb4ab1-bdc6-52df-894d-4e715c654bd8"] :activity/first-step "step-3f47cbab-f727-53bf-9a6c-904dcbb4215a" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-3f47cbab-f727-53bf-9a6c-904dcbb4215a" :step/id #uuid "3f47cbab-f727-53bf-9a6c-904dcbb4215a" :step/content "question-17beca86-e4d7-5c67-a86e-43d1aa96f62a" :step/next "step-56ceaeb1-6ec7-595a-9937-934df0bd5805" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-56ceaeb1-6ec7-595a-9937-934df0bd5805" :step/id #uuid "56ceaeb1-6ec7-595a-9937-934df0bd5805" :step/content 17592186284454 :step/next "step-75d99a5b-c852-5111-a02c-67dea933b6f4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-75d99a5b-c852-5111-a02c-67dea933b6f4" :step/id #uuid "75d99a5b-c852-5111-a02c-67dea933b6f4" :step/content 17592186378892 :step/next "step-05f9a82f-656b-50bc-8f0f-e8191e8f5a42" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-05f9a82f-656b-50bc-8f0f-e8191e8f5a42" :step/id #uuid "05f9a82f-656b-50bc-8f0f-e8191e8f5a42" :step/content 17592186401460 :step/next "step-8beb4ab1-bdc6-52df-894d-4e715c654bd8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8beb4ab1-bdc6-52df-894d-4e715c654bd8" :step/id #uuid "8beb4ab1-bdc6-52df-894d-4e715c654bd8" :step/content 17592186378941 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166758 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d97aad73-981d-50e0-a955-015b210a6f0f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d97aad73-981d-50e0-a955-015b210a6f0f" :question/id #uuid "d97aad73-981d-50e0-a955-015b210a6f0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-95641" :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\int \\frac{{e}^{6}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{e}^{6}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x={e}^{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x={e}^{6}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7810ca7c-722f-58fa-afb8-be8fc3259c20"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7810ca7c-722f-58fa-afb8-be8fc3259c20" :answer-field/id #uuid "7810ca7c-722f-58fa-afb8-be8fc3259c20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1447d267-1e94-59e7-8cc2-bdf68d102cf6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7dc3b388-1d6a-5377-bbfd-7e690bfaab10" "answer-f88511bb-4494-56fe-8ace-2548c1b0cb89" "answer-660f35ec-7422-55ff-a4b9-a742d72bc40d" "answer-2456e542-be97-5604-8295-9ddeb7151992" "answer-1447d267-1e94-59e7-8cc2-bdf68d102cf6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7dc3b388-1d6a-5377-bbfd-7e690bfaab10" :answer/id #uuid "7dc3b388-1d6a-5377-bbfd-7e690bfaab10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{e}^{6}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f88511bb-4494-56fe-8ace-2548c1b0cb89" :answer/id #uuid "f88511bb-4494-56fe-8ace-2548c1b0cb89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{e}^{6}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-660f35ec-7422-55ff-a4b9-a742d72bc40d" :answer/id #uuid "660f35ec-7422-55ff-a4b9-a742d72bc40d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡6x+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2456e542-be97-5604-8295-9ddeb7151992" :answer/id #uuid "2456e542-be97-5604-8295-9ddeb7151992" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1447d267-1e94-59e7-8cc2-bdf68d102cf6" :answer/id #uuid "1447d267-1e94-59e7-8cc2-bdf68d102cf6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{6}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166759 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a85c39f1-bee3-513f-a104-f73814b0e91c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a85c39f1-bee3-513f-a104-f73814b0e91c" :question/id #uuid "a85c39f1-bee3-513f-a104-f73814b0e91c" :question/math-academy-id "q-95646" :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\int (\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}+1)\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}+1)\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x+x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d0e7872f-98e0-55e8-bef0-ccee58ebf724"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d0e7872f-98e0-55e8-bef0-ccee58ebf724" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0e7872f-98e0-55e8-bef0-ccee58ebf724" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2e9b8cdb-c159-5120-908b-13dd4a85f1cd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c5b01a17-36c9-5b62-a7f5-e79f1f6d940d" "answer-17c51a7f-b34d-5851-906e-2441cf3fa2b8" "answer-2e9b8cdb-c159-5120-908b-13dd4a85f1cd" "answer-51c37b7d-7939-589a-acc8-fd7d126126f9" "answer-25f06daa-ca4a-5dc5-bd3b-da45960b77ca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5b01a17-36c9-5b62-a7f5-e79f1f6d940d" :answer/id #uuid "c5b01a17-36c9-5b62-a7f5-e79f1f6d940d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x+x+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17c51a7f-b34d-5851-906e-2441cf3fa2b8" :answer/id #uuid "17c51a7f-b34d-5851-906e-2441cf3fa2b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2x+x+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e9b8cdb-c159-5120-908b-13dd4a85f1cd" :answer/id #uuid "2e9b8cdb-c159-5120-908b-13dd4a85f1cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x+x+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51c37b7d-7939-589a-acc8-fd7d126126f9" :answer/id #uuid "51c37b7d-7939-589a-acc8-fd7d126126f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x+x+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25f06daa-ca4a-5dc5-bd3b-da45960b77ca" :answer/id #uuid "25f06daa-ca4a-5dc5-bd3b-da45960b77ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡2x+x+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-d6793e73-a4eb-50c8-9916-d08f03ca36cd" :activity/id #uuid "d6793e73-a4eb-50c8-9916-d08f03ca36cd" :activity/title "Integration Using Inverse Hyperbolic Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048979 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-d9e84051-9672-5ee7-9111-0fa0bc478887" "step-df5b6f93-2d34-5728-bd0b-f9f795729120" "step-fd1a936d-1667-500d-bb90-9d559b94e793" "step-8f8d29fb-7b44-5458-8d2c-9544a666d363" "step-5791a324-5a7e-58db-af53-d97fe8e49fad"] :activity/first-step "step-d9e84051-9672-5ee7-9111-0fa0bc478887" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-d9e84051-9672-5ee7-9111-0fa0bc478887" :step/id #uuid "d9e84051-9672-5ee7-9111-0fa0bc478887" :step/content 17592186167340 :step/next "step-df5b6f93-2d34-5728-bd0b-f9f795729120" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-df5b6f93-2d34-5728-bd0b-f9f795729120" :step/id #uuid "df5b6f93-2d34-5728-bd0b-f9f795729120" :step/content "question-d97aad73-981d-50e0-a955-015b210a6f0f" :step/next "step-fd1a936d-1667-500d-bb90-9d559b94e793" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-fd1a936d-1667-500d-bb90-9d559b94e793" :step/id #uuid "fd1a936d-1667-500d-bb90-9d559b94e793" :step/content "question-a85c39f1-bee3-513f-a104-f73814b0e91c" :step/next "step-8f8d29fb-7b44-5458-8d2c-9544a666d363" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8f8d29fb-7b44-5458-8d2c-9544a666d363" :step/id #uuid "8f8d29fb-7b44-5458-8d2c-9544a666d363" :step/content 17592186360617 :step/next "step-5791a324-5a7e-58db-af53-d97fe8e49fad" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5791a324-5a7e-58db-af53-d97fe8e49fad" :step/id #uuid "5791a324-5a7e-58db-af53-d97fe8e49fad" :step/content 17592186360618 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166913 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fa1fc563-4426-5908-b1bd-3188967331c8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-fa1fc563-4426-5908-b1bd-3188967331c8" :question/id #uuid "fa1fc563-4426-5908-b1bd-3188967331c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-332719" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1cd11c63564050d5851c53e6a808e6a7b73ee33ce6cbe77c9278c6a3429eaec5.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8d768191-43c2-52ef-8bd4-abb5c4c7e134"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8d768191-43c2-52ef-8bd4-abb5c4c7e134" :answer-field/id #uuid "8d768191-43c2-52ef-8bd4-abb5c4c7e134" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c944dd89-80c1-5096-989e-9c5466682fc3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c944dd89-80c1-5096-989e-9c5466682fc3" "answer-4a6a586f-0cf2-5116-a740-1a9d8f1242c4" "answer-88a6fa17-dfc1-5da9-b77a-35d12299dbb0" "answer-cd2ca45a-40c5-5ab4-aa0e-968f3478e0f2" "answer-08ff6638-43e0-5544-89de-9608b0711546"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c944dd89-80c1-5096-989e-9c5466682fc3" :answer/id #uuid "c944dd89-80c1-5096-989e-9c5466682fc3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a6a586f-0cf2-5116-a740-1a9d8f1242c4" :answer/id #uuid "4a6a586f-0cf2-5116-a740-1a9d8f1242c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88a6fa17-dfc1-5da9-b77a-35d12299dbb0" :answer/id #uuid "88a6fa17-dfc1-5da9-b77a-35d12299dbb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd2ca45a-40c5-5ab4-aa0e-968f3478e0f2" :answer/id #uuid "cd2ca45a-40c5-5ab4-aa0e-968f3478e0f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08ff6638-43e0-5544-89de-9608b0711546" :answer/id #uuid "08ff6638-43e0-5544-89de-9608b0711546" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167235 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-4t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/a2/a2196ea04e12f3c706300e0c9d15806606e408f5e582d3e4f2b9c62f0efc343f.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-2t})^{′}}=\\frac{-4{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}}{-2{c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}}{{c}_{1}}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/14/1429eda4336d47aec06c7dd673ff6163203423739c520fd0c83db1c5cb072ac7.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164469 :answer-field/correct 17592186164471 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "activity-8170d687-2150-5ce0-9fbc-f1d2e19d49be" :activity/id #uuid "8170d687-2150-5ce0-9fbc-f1d2e19d49be" :activity/title "Phase Portraits for Decoupled Linear Systems" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048395 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-1f042b9b-5541-5e4f-946e-597ca617d6fd" "step-ae3c91ec-0646-58d2-a00b-c0e68120022f" "step-a8502bd6-d620-5db8-94f8-350537638008" "step-8198ac8e-1e9d-5686-bcf7-2cb7faeeb3bc" "step-7590c9da-e337-57de-aa4b-d285843f0aa4"] :activity/first-step "step-1f042b9b-5541-5e4f-946e-597ca617d6fd" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-1f042b9b-5541-5e4f-946e-597ca617d6fd" :step/id #uuid "1f042b9b-5541-5e4f-946e-597ca617d6fd" :step/content 17592186305101 :step/next "step-ae3c91ec-0646-58d2-a00b-c0e68120022f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ae3c91ec-0646-58d2-a00b-c0e68120022f" :step/id #uuid "ae3c91ec-0646-58d2-a00b-c0e68120022f" :step/content "question-fa1fc563-4426-5908-b1bd-3188967331c8" :step/next "step-a8502bd6-d620-5db8-94f8-350537638008" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a8502bd6-d620-5db8-94f8-350537638008" :step/id #uuid "a8502bd6-d620-5db8-94f8-350537638008" :step/content 17592186167235 :step/next "step-8198ac8e-1e9d-5686-bcf7-2cb7faeeb3bc" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8198ac8e-1e9d-5686-bcf7-2cb7faeeb3bc" :step/id #uuid "8198ac8e-1e9d-5686-bcf7-2cb7faeeb3bc" :step/content 17592186308405 :step/next "step-7590c9da-e337-57de-aa4b-d285843f0aa4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7590c9da-e337-57de-aa4b-d285843f0aa4" :step/id #uuid "7590c9da-e337-57de-aa4b-d285843f0aa4" :step/content 17592186391169 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147595 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-90c33764-1c3e-51f1-880d-65fb7c77f172"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-90c33764-1c3e-51f1-880d-65fb7c77f172" :question/id #uuid "90c33764-1c3e-51f1-880d-65fb7c77f172" :question/math-academy-id "q-61194" :question/problem "In a particular year, firefighters in a city responded to $40$ emergencies. The following year, firefighters attended $165%$ of the total number of emergencies compared to the previous year. How many emergencies did firefighters attend in the second year?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of emergencies that firefighters attended in the first year, which is $40$ emergencies. The percentage is $165%,$ and the part is the total number of emergencies firefighters attended in the second year.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n165%=165÷100=1.65\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =165%\\times 40 \\\\  & =1.65\\times 40 \\\\  & =66\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $165%$ of $40$ is $66.$ So, firefighters attended $66$ emergencies in the second year.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n165%=\\frac{165}{100}=\\frac{165÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{33}{20}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =165%\\times 40 \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{20}\\times 40 \\\\  & =33\\times \\frac{40}{20} \\\\  & =33\\times 2 \\\\  & =66\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $165%$ of $40$ is $66.$ So, firefighters attended $66$ emergencies in the second year." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c30bdd72-4d41-5214-a1f5-29955bfd7ec1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c30bdd72-4d41-5214-a1f5-29955bfd7ec1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c30bdd72-4d41-5214-a1f5-29955bfd7ec1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-043a9e3e-b3cd-5cfa-9d81-e26ad450d6d7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fab2505d-f4e8-5303-98fc-f5bf2a45cf35" "answer-043a9e3e-b3cd-5cfa-9d81-e26ad450d6d7" "answer-ea7c30f7-8e5e-5379-ac16-2ec9b3bc8b9f" "answer-a261e6f2-0933-593a-835f-6a1e14079910" "answer-7ea86345-e78c-5f38-b314-bdcdcd13141f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fab2505d-f4e8-5303-98fc-f5bf2a45cf35" :answer/id #uuid "fab2505d-f4e8-5303-98fc-f5bf2a45cf35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "61" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-043a9e3e-b3cd-5cfa-9d81-e26ad450d6d7" :answer/id #uuid "043a9e3e-b3cd-5cfa-9d81-e26ad450d6d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "66" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea7c30f7-8e5e-5379-ac16-2ec9b3bc8b9f" :answer/id #uuid "ea7c30f7-8e5e-5379-ac16-2ec9b3bc8b9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "69" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a261e6f2-0933-593a-835f-6a1e14079910" :answer/id #uuid "a261e6f2-0933-593a-835f-6a1e14079910" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "72" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ea86345-e78c-5f38-b314-bdcdcd13141f" :answer/id #uuid "7ea86345-e78c-5f38-b314-bdcdcd13141f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "73" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179824 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-81ed1269-d411-55d0-9278-76332ca965f4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-81ed1269-d411-55d0-9278-76332ca965f4" :question/id #uuid "81ed1269-d411-55d0-9278-76332ca965f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-75654" :question/problem "$(-2)\\cdot (-8.6)=$" :question/worked-solution "We have the product of two negative numbers, which gives a positive number.\n\n$$\n(-2)\\cdot (-8.6)=2\\cdot 8.6=17.2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ccdc240d-6ac0-5c24-9e5f-9b1fb2cab436"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ccdc240d-6ac0-5c24-9e5f-9b1fb2cab436" :answer-field/id #uuid "ccdc240d-6ac0-5c24-9e5f-9b1fb2cab436" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-34be3dd1-5cf2-50b3-b40c-6ae74c0269f4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d35a1a7e-0f9d-5ee0-af7e-39ff18bddc90" "answer-30958349-350a-5a29-a374-a3c26918b0b8" "answer-1f23f63d-1c73-5eeb-8f85-ff4c5f9bb1cc" "answer-34be3dd1-5cf2-50b3-b40c-6ae74c0269f4" "answer-4f858313-2e3a-5cc8-830d-e8df4c9e098f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d35a1a7e-0f9d-5ee0-af7e-39ff18bddc90" :answer/id #uuid "d35a1a7e-0f9d-5ee0-af7e-39ff18bddc90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-17.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-30958349-350a-5a29-a374-a3c26918b0b8" :answer/id #uuid "30958349-350a-5a29-a374-a3c26918b0b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f23f63d-1c73-5eeb-8f85-ff4c5f9bb1cc" :answer/id #uuid "1f23f63d-1c73-5eeb-8f85-ff4c5f9bb1cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34be3dd1-5cf2-50b3-b40c-6ae74c0269f4" :answer/id #uuid "34be3dd1-5cf2-50b3-b40c-6ae74c0269f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "17.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f858313-2e3a-5cc8-830d-e8df4c9e098f" :answer/id #uuid "4f858313-2e3a-5cc8-830d-e8df4c9e098f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073899 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8f463b76-07fb-5ba1-955a-1c27fbeaf1d4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-8f463b76-07fb-5ba1-955a-1c27fbeaf1d4" :question/id #uuid "8f463b76-07fb-5ba1-955a-1c27fbeaf1d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-61272" :question/problem "What is the distance between the points $(0,-2)$  and $(0,-4)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/9e/9edd6fb0f5b8dbf861a10cb559e597abaee54d4f833735912ab8ecb8b6e6091a.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $2$ units, so the distance equals $2.$\n\n![](images/ee/ee815ded554b01fd73cfd3577cfd7a21ea1360a32414de62a2f43f8b8612fd67.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/19/19d94e1c26e61bfccdbab7083781ea5fb164910c54719135b348a60002c27a0b.png)\n\nThe point $(0,-2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(0,-4)$ is at a distance of $4$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $4-2=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fe1cad92-38bd-52de-a7fd-ee07bf798a45"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fe1cad92-38bd-52de-a7fd-ee07bf798a45" :answer-field/id #uuid "fe1cad92-38bd-52de-a7fd-ee07bf798a45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-db61b73a-6ab9-56d5-8f1b-246a5b28b8d8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-74343b0c-fe77-58ec-a95f-54b3006fc19b" "answer-db61b73a-6ab9-56d5-8f1b-246a5b28b8d8" "answer-8b054765-177a-507b-aced-090f3a91ac77" "answer-5a9f768d-3b7a-50b9-ac43-fb1ac35f78d8" "answer-c22d2239-9d96-5d3d-b39b-6e4294bf44b1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74343b0c-fe77-58ec-a95f-54b3006fc19b" :answer/id #uuid "74343b0c-fe77-58ec-a95f-54b3006fc19b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db61b73a-6ab9-56d5-8f1b-246a5b28b8d8" :answer/id #uuid "db61b73a-6ab9-56d5-8f1b-246a5b28b8d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b054765-177a-507b-aced-090f3a91ac77" :answer/id #uuid "8b054765-177a-507b-aced-090f3a91ac77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a9f768d-3b7a-50b9-ac43-fb1ac35f78d8" :answer/id #uuid "5a9f768d-3b7a-50b9-ac43-fb1ac35f78d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c22d2239-9d96-5d3d-b39b-6e4294bf44b1" :answer/id #uuid "c22d2239-9d96-5d3d-b39b-6e4294bf44b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110675 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5af7e394-12e7-5f3a-b066-84aff669a022"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-5af7e394-12e7-5f3a-b066-84aff669a022" :question/id #uuid "5af7e394-12e7-5f3a-b066-84aff669a022" :question/math-academy-id "q-155030" :question/problem "![](images/95/95726fe45adfcf88fb3d2380779380fb236a5dd0a3bb332847130a8cb5251251.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams above shows three collinear points?" :question/worked-solution "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Diagram I shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/61/611d93edb119e6bdc60bc3492b72d2c08730ef946232e3c3189b7d04eab43c18.png)\n\n- Diagram II shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](images/32/32bb229224aac8d0b8feda0160c00d7afa9aa42827f448834a173b926a9dd48b.png)\n\n \n\n- Diagram III shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](images/38/3886d294defeb0a4e43c5612c981b6b392a23067c0ecb51ebab6c2dab154c218.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ac8cfa71-a98c-57db-8d46-6eba8f2a87b9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ac8cfa71-a98c-57db-8d46-6eba8f2a87b9" :answer-field/id #uuid "ac8cfa71-a98c-57db-8d46-6eba8f2a87b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f8ab19ae-f23f-5e56-a623-ff1afaf77f6d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aa073262-2b05-5652-a10b-edbf70e1bcb4" "answer-f8ab19ae-f23f-5e56-a623-ff1afaf77f6d" "answer-25290c47-5501-5d05-bf5f-86c572554236" "answer-1d6e006a-3651-55e2-82ca-b07d0cbd9083" "answer-cd55077e-30a6-57f4-96d5-cbe0cbc13b2d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa073262-2b05-5652-a10b-edbf70e1bcb4" :answer/id #uuid "aa073262-2b05-5652-a10b-edbf70e1bcb4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8ab19ae-f23f-5e56-a623-ff1afaf77f6d" :answer/id #uuid "f8ab19ae-f23f-5e56-a623-ff1afaf77f6d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25290c47-5501-5d05-bf5f-86c572554236" :answer/id #uuid "25290c47-5501-5d05-bf5f-86c572554236" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d6e006a-3651-55e2-82ca-b07d0cbd9083" :answer/id #uuid "1d6e006a-3651-55e2-82ca-b07d0cbd9083" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd55077e-30a6-57f4-96d5-cbe0cbc13b2d" :answer/id #uuid "cd55077e-30a6-57f4-96d5-cbe0cbc13b2d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106198 :question/problem "![](images/59/597626d534c99880de4eabaf51be1c756149395ce285e843a9eaa9a8bdc395d8.png)\n\nWhich vertices are adjacent to $E?$" :question/worked-solution "$E$ is the endpoint of the sides $\\overset{―}{DE}$ and $\\overset{―}{EF}.$ Therefore, the vertices $D$ and $F$ are adjacent to $E.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186103064 :answer-field/correct 17592186103067 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152125 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-20ed3ba1-b1a8-58f4-bd28-e42bcc1fa66a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-20ed3ba1-b1a8-58f4-bd28-e42bcc1fa66a" :question/id #uuid "20ed3ba1-b1a8-58f4-bd28-e42bcc1fa66a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98373" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are integers?\n\nI. $-1$\n\nII. $0$\n\nIII. $1$" :question/worked-solution "The integers consist of all positive and negative whole numbers and zero.\n\n$$\n…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…\n$$\n\n \n\nOf the given numbers, $-1,$ $0,$ and $1$ are all integers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-af925059-3a5f-5fa3-90c5-752a6f343cbd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-af925059-3a5f-5fa3-90c5-752a6f343cbd" :answer-field/id #uuid "af925059-3a5f-5fa3-90c5-752a6f343cbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-faf857a2-8623-5564-a455-5ce3b85b40b9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4bc9243a-1e45-596c-96d5-d7386c09bd5e" "answer-d7844ae8-7dce-53bc-ab13-f84339c7bc68" "answer-faf857a2-8623-5564-a455-5ce3b85b40b9" "answer-09cfbfe2-a49a-5d22-b4e1-802ad451ab15" "answer-0da4e9f1-ea41-5305-8ea0-e01a52047060"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4bc9243a-1e45-596c-96d5-d7386c09bd5e" :answer/id #uuid "4bc9243a-1e45-596c-96d5-d7386c09bd5e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7844ae8-7dce-53bc-ab13-f84339c7bc68" :answer/id #uuid "d7844ae8-7dce-53bc-ab13-f84339c7bc68" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-faf857a2-8623-5564-a455-5ce3b85b40b9" :answer/id #uuid "faf857a2-8623-5564-a455-5ce3b85b40b9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09cfbfe2-a49a-5d22-b4e1-802ad451ab15" :answer/id #uuid "09cfbfe2-a49a-5d22-b4e1-802ad451ab15" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0da4e9f1-ea41-5305-8ea0-e01a52047060" :answer/id #uuid "0da4e9f1-ea41-5305-8ea0-e01a52047060" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073739 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-63fb796f-6e2e-5f84-a08a-5f5d5b996ee4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-63fb796f-6e2e-5f84-a08a-5f5d5b996ee4" :question/id #uuid "63fb796f-6e2e-5f84-a08a-5f5d5b996ee4" :question/math-academy-id "q-236393" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e612ac8882e5abbe9f5eff3fc9f6e1f9e5e5d5827f01d232532ca8378c508793.png)\n\nExpress the number of hexagons to the total number of shapes as a ratio in its simplest form.\n\n{{field-1}}\n$:$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We count the number of hexagons. We see that there are $4$ hexagons.\n\nWe count the total number of shapes. We see that there are $8$ shapes in total.\n\nSo the ratio of hexagons to total shapes is $4:8.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers in a ratio by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,4 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,8 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of hexagons to the total shapes is $1:2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-46a1e1ec-6fc7-5470-9953-6afd6d16c7d8" "answer-field-21c0a936-65d3-544b-823a-d54356a48ef4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-46a1e1ec-6fc7-5470-9953-6afd6d16c7d8" :answer-field/id #uuid "46a1e1ec-6fc7-5470-9953-6afd6d16c7d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-50e6fea6-9b42-5ed3-b05b-ca82f193ee2e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-50e6fea6-9b42-5ed3-b05b-ca82f193ee2e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50e6fea6-9b42-5ed3-b05b-ca82f193ee2e" :answer/id #uuid "50e6fea6-9b42-5ed3-b05b-ca82f193ee2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-21c0a936-65d3-544b-823a-d54356a48ef4" :answer-field/id #uuid "21c0a936-65d3-544b-823a-d54356a48ef4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-060cb9e2-a062-5d4e-b79a-46882843668c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-060cb9e2-a062-5d4e-b79a-46882843668c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-060cb9e2-a062-5d4e-b79a-46882843668c" :answer/id #uuid "060cb9e2-a062-5d4e-b79a-46882843668c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064256 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bd178561-a381-5104-8041-a4f6b40537cb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-bd178561-a381-5104-8041-a4f6b40537cb" :question/id #uuid "bd178561-a381-5104-8041-a4f6b40537cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-128106" :question/problem "$201-249=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $249$ is larger than $201,$ the answer will be negative.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem:\n\n$$\n249-201\n$$\n\nNext, we carry out the subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n249-201=48.\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n201-249=-48.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b01b6f43-db85-5f44-a21c-7bc482e17f50"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b01b6f43-db85-5f44-a21c-7bc482e17f50" :answer-field/id #uuid "b01b6f43-db85-5f44-a21c-7bc482e17f50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4094f0d0-05cf-5ee4-a732-943e61a72904" :answer-field/choices ["answer-312c0471-f6a8-584a-8540-c0ffccf50082" "answer-4094f0d0-05cf-5ee4-a732-943e61a72904" "answer-dba740cd-f7de-5931-a8ed-ffa787631d12" "answer-a1dfcbed-4a13-5c15-bf37-f46225c5f0fc" "answer-87f8df68-bd78-54f9-a792-5d87d762bda8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-312c0471-f6a8-584a-8540-c0ffccf50082" :answer/id #uuid "312c0471-f6a8-584a-8540-c0ffccf50082" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-38" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4094f0d0-05cf-5ee4-a732-943e61a72904" :answer/id #uuid "4094f0d0-05cf-5ee4-a732-943e61a72904" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dba740cd-f7de-5931-a8ed-ffa787631d12" :answer/id #uuid "dba740cd-f7de-5931-a8ed-ffa787631d12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1dfcbed-4a13-5c15-bf37-f46225c5f0fc" :answer/id #uuid "a1dfcbed-4a13-5c15-bf37-f46225c5f0fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87f8df68-bd78-54f9-a792-5d87d762bda8" :answer/id #uuid "87f8df68-bd78-54f9-a792-5d87d762bda8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064564 :question/problem "$(-18)÷6=$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we are dividing a negative number $(-18)$ by a positive one $(6).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n$$\n(-18)÷6=-\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $18$ by $6$ without any signs: $18÷6=3.$ Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n(-18)÷6=-3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186061579 :answer-field/correct 17592186061583 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054800 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8a3d8f81-e9cc-5df6-a543-4a7637a4bf37"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-8a3d8f81-e9cc-5df6-a543-4a7637a4bf37" :question/id #uuid "8a3d8f81-e9cc-5df6-a543-4a7637a4bf37" :question/math-academy-id "q-108484" :question/problem "Translate the following phrase into an algebraic expression: \"Eleven more than a number.\"" :question/worked-solution "If we denote our number by $x$, then the phrase \"eleven more than a number\" can be written as\n\n$$\nx+11.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9e5d655d-fbf8-5d56-ba34-b7364dcf679e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9e5d655d-fbf8-5d56-ba34-b7364dcf679e" :answer-field/id #uuid "9e5d655d-fbf8-5d56-ba34-b7364dcf679e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ecda9f85-7385-56c8-8d13-6935cfbd7169" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3b428f3f-5b16-53f0-90ce-5a5f77c7668b" "answer-cb21fb7c-771e-59ab-859e-8244b0f384ff" "answer-c4573cdc-fa07-5d0e-a201-5b0f200109dd" "answer-ecda9f85-7385-56c8-8d13-6935cfbd7169" "answer-13e534db-9596-59d9-9c6b-3930ced0eda8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b428f3f-5b16-53f0-90ce-5a5f77c7668b" :answer/id #uuid "3b428f3f-5b16-53f0-90ce-5a5f77c7668b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb21fb7c-771e-59ab-859e-8244b0f384ff" :answer/id #uuid "cb21fb7c-771e-59ab-859e-8244b0f384ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11+" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4573cdc-fa07-5d0e-a201-5b0f200109dd" :answer/id #uuid "c4573cdc-fa07-5d0e-a201-5b0f200109dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecda9f85-7385-56c8-8d13-6935cfbd7169" :answer/id #uuid "ecda9f85-7385-56c8-8d13-6935cfbd7169" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13e534db-9596-59d9-9c6b-3930ced0eda8" :answer/id #uuid "13e534db-9596-59d9-9c6b-3930ced0eda8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172603 :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $14\\,733\\,189$\n\nII. $1\\,589\\,610$\n\nIII. $873\\,100\\,584$" :question/worked-solution "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$\n\n- The number $14\\,733\\,189$ is not divisible by $2,$ so it is not divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- The number $1\\,589\\,610$ is divisible by $2,$ and it is also divisible by $3$ since the sum of its digits is divisible by $3:$\n\n$$\n1+5+8+9+6+1+0=30\n$$\n\nSo, $1\\,589\\,610$ is divisible by $6.$\n\n- The number $873\\,100\\,584$ is divisible by $2,$ and it is also divisible by $3$ since the sum of its digits is divisible by $3:$\n\n$$\n8+7+3+1+0+0+5+8+4=36\n$$\n\nSo, $873\\,100\\,584$ is divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171243 :answer-field/correct 17592186171248 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073880 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ca5fdebc-3254-5299-89b6-95fbebb635d8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ca5fdebc-3254-5299-89b6-95fbebb635d8" :question/id #uuid "ca5fdebc-3254-5299-89b6-95fbebb635d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-60248" :question/problem "From left to right, what are the missing numbers in the following sentence?\n\nIn a liquid, there are $\\frac{2}{3}$ quarts of honey for each quart of water. Therefore, the liquid has a ratio of $\\boxed{}$ quarts of honey to $\\boxed{}$ quarts of water." :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite a fraction $\\frac{2}{3}$ as a ratio:\n\n$$\n2:3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the liquid has a ratio of $2$ quarts of honey to $3$ quarts of water." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e7f79f08-c364-55a5-b2d4-7a59020d9e10"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e7f79f08-c364-55a5-b2d4-7a59020d9e10" :answer-field/id #uuid "e7f79f08-c364-55a5-b2d4-7a59020d9e10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correc