{:db/id "step-bdc8647f-7c30-5355-b6b1-d2005ce98062" :step/id #uuid "bdc8647f-7c30-5355-b6b1-d2005ce98062" :step/content "question-8a3d8f81-e9cc-5df6-a543-4a7637a4bf37" :step/next "step-e78ffec5-1b19-5a7e-8f25-192954cf695a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e78ffec5-1b19-5a7e-8f25-192954cf695a" :step/id #uuid "e78ffec5-1b19-5a7e-8f25-192954cf695a" :step/content 17592186172603 :step/next "step-a18b17f2-41c0-5be0-97e7-71dcdae9c073" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a18b17f2-41c0-5be0-97e7-71dcdae9c073" :step/id #uuid "a18b17f2-41c0-5be0-97e7-71dcdae9c073" :step/content "question-ca5fdebc-3254-5299-89b6-95fbebb635d8" :step/next "step-91e98bad-7979-59e7-936e-702de839d0d6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-91e98bad-7979-59e7-936e-702de839d0d6" :step/id #uuid "91e98bad-7979-59e7-936e-702de839d0d6" :step/content "question-df0053ff-176e-53fc-ba84-2cd6292eecda" :step/next "step-f0fccd66-fa86-5d15-88d0-2060e8fb42fb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f0fccd66-fa86-5d15-88d0-2060e8fb42fb" :step/id #uuid "f0fccd66-fa86-5d15-88d0-2060e8fb42fb" :step/content "question-27d1fbdb-2975-5b0f-9d8e-58c27611f8b1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166847 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-57998d9d-2f79-5e0e-981a-e73726e34a06" "question-113bec1d-2c81-56b8-a44e-d46424e8b73b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166877 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e178509e-4821-5890-a867-11dacf4cab24" "question-110563bd-a4e1-5dc9-8400-b537447d4a3c" "question-f7a1a1db-a551-5627-b716-a412f2910020"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167116 :question/problem "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{3}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0.5, &  \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0.6, & P({A}_{2})=0.2 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.1, & P({A}_{3})=0.7\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=3,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}) & =1-(P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})) \\\\  & =1-(0.2+0.7) \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{3}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.1)(0.7)}{(0.5)(0.1)+(0.6)(0.2)+(0.1)(0.7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.07}{0.24} \\\\  & ≈0.29.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163774 :answer-field/correct 17592186163776 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-57998d9d-2f79-5e0e-981a-e73726e34a06" :question/id #uuid "57998d9d-2f79-5e0e-981a-e73726e34a06" :question/math-academy-id "q-147890" :question/problem "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{2}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0.25, & P({A}_{1})=0.3 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=1, & P({A}_{2})=0.3 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.34, & P({A}_{3})=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=3,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})=0.3+0.3+0.4=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{2}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)(0.3)}{(0.25)(0.3)+(1)(0.3)+(0.34)(0.4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.3}{0.511} \\\\  & ≈0.587,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cf2d06bc-9b8b-504e-b75b-69a639c7f6d8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cf2d06bc-9b8b-504e-b75b-69a639c7f6d8" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf2d06bc-9b8b-504e-b75b-69a639c7f6d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f2807406-4c6e-55a8-869c-9ed22fd08234" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bb1ca141-31af-5804-82f6-a6ee0dbea30c" "answer-d8a87763-e568-5dd0-9ec5-b9e11588c846" "answer-acf7b9d9-63c7-5c0a-9f42-2a96d3ac6eaf" "answer-f2807406-4c6e-55a8-869c-9ed22fd08234" "answer-f2c24486-d922-54f0-b54c-67f92ead900e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb1ca141-31af-5804-82f6-a6ee0dbea30c" :answer/id #uuid "bb1ca141-31af-5804-82f6-a6ee0dbea30c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.149" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8a87763-e568-5dd0-9ec5-b9e11588c846" :answer/id #uuid "d8a87763-e568-5dd0-9ec5-b9e11588c846" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-acf7b9d9-63c7-5c0a-9f42-2a96d3ac6eaf" :answer/id #uuid "acf7b9d9-63c7-5c0a-9f42-2a96d3ac6eaf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.489" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2807406-4c6e-55a8-869c-9ed22fd08234" :answer/id #uuid "f2807406-4c6e-55a8-869c-9ed22fd08234" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.587" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2c24486-d922-54f0-b54c-67f92ead900e" :answer/id #uuid "f2c24486-d922-54f0-b54c-67f92ead900e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-113bec1d-2c81-56b8-a44e-d46424e8b73b" :question/id #uuid "113bec1d-2c81-56b8-a44e-d46424e8b73b" :question/math-academy-id "q-147883" :question/problem "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{2}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0.1, & P({A}_{1})=0.15 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0.2, & P({A}_{2})=0.46 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.6, & P({A}_{3})=0.39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=3,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})=0.15+0.46+0.39=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{2}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.2)(0.46)}{(0.1)(0.15)+(0.2)(0.46)+(0.6)(0.39)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.092}{0.341} \\\\  & ≈0.270,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-688a8d86-a2ba-5165-80ac-f7c7083df837"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-688a8d86-a2ba-5165-80ac-f7c7083df837" :answer-field/id #uuid "688a8d86-a2ba-5165-80ac-f7c7083df837" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f83de6e5-577e-5eed-b1f6-2f23a750133f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b1748b03-34f2-57c0-ab18-c9ff2c94eeff" "answer-f83de6e5-577e-5eed-b1f6-2f23a750133f" "answer-9ad8eb5f-def8-529e-bcc6-a2ad769082ba" "answer-90071b27-b3b3-5f67-a5b6-55ed06803ddc" "answer-782df96d-2604-59c7-88af-7986ccdf4161"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1748b03-34f2-57c0-ab18-c9ff2c94eeff" :answer/id #uuid "b1748b03-34f2-57c0-ab18-c9ff2c94eeff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.263" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f83de6e5-577e-5eed-b1f6-2f23a750133f" :answer/id #uuid "f83de6e5-577e-5eed-b1f6-2f23a750133f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.270" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ad8eb5f-def8-529e-bcc6-a2ad769082ba" :answer/id #uuid "9ad8eb5f-def8-529e-bcc6-a2ad769082ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.277" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90071b27-b3b3-5f67-a5b6-55ed06803ddc" :answer/id #uuid "90071b27-b3b3-5f67-a5b6-55ed06803ddc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.281" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-782df96d-2604-59c7-88af-7986ccdf4161" :answer/id #uuid "782df96d-2604-59c7-88af-7986ccdf4161" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.289" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e178509e-4821-5890-a867-11dacf4cab24" :question/id #uuid "e178509e-4821-5890-a867-11dacf4cab24" :question/math-academy-id "q-148073" :question/problem "Apples produced by a particular farm are of three varieties: $1,2,$ and $3.$\n\n- $30%$ of the apples are of variety $1,$ and $45%$ of those apples weight more than $200\\,g.$\n\n- $20%$ of the apples are of variety $2,$ and $15%$ of those apples weight more than $200\\,g.$\n\n- $50%$ of the apples are of variety $3,$ and $10%$ of those apples weight more than $200\\,g.$\n\nIf $75%$ of apples from the farm are sold to a local supermarket, and $20%$ of apples sold to the supermarket weigh more than $200\\,g,$ what is the probability that a randomly chosen apple that weighs more than $200\\,g$ is sold to the supermarket? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be the event that an apple is sold to the supermarket, $W$ be the event that the apple weights more than $200\\,g,$ and ${V}_{1},{V}_{2},{V}_{2}$ be the events that an apple is of variety $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({V}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(W|{V}_{1})=0.45 \\\\  & P({V}_{2})=0.2 &  & P(W|{V}_{2})=0.15 \\\\  & P({V}_{3})=0.5 &  & P(W|{V}_{3})=0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${V}_{1}⊔{V}_{2}⊔{V}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({V}_{1})+P({V}_{2})+P({V}_{3})=0.3+0.2+0.5=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $75%$ of apples from the farm are sold to a local supermarket, we have\n\n$$\nP(S)=0.75.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of apples sold to the supermarket weigh more than $200\\,g,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(W|S)=0.2.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen apple that weigh more than $200\\,g$ is sold to the supermarket, which is represented as $P(S|W).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S|W) & =\\frac{P(W|S)P(S)}{P(W)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(W|S)P(S)}{P(W|{V}_{1})P({V}_{1})+P(W|{V}_{2})P({V}_{2})+P(W|{V}_{3})P({V}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.2)(0.75)}{(0.3)(0.45)+(0.2)(0.15)+(0.5)(0.1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.15}{0.215} \\\\  & ≈0.698,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b5194c2f-a7d5-587b-94a9-0a5d5e9abd11"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b5194c2f-a7d5-587b-94a9-0a5d5e9abd11" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5194c2f-a7d5-587b-94a9-0a5d5e9abd11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ca1eb351-55b5-56cf-8dcd-fb494fd3356f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b987a4d5-25a4-5ccd-91bb-03cded3463b7" "answer-a76bc466-a99c-5979-a45e-6300e8bad947" "answer-141226aa-99b9-5761-b841-c0e97cf724ee" "answer-ca1eb351-55b5-56cf-8dcd-fb494fd3356f" "answer-1a1c4477-0150-5af4-b2d4-f4066d364834"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b987a4d5-25a4-5ccd-91bb-03cded3463b7" :answer/id #uuid "b987a4d5-25a4-5ccd-91bb-03cded3463b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.558" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a76bc466-a99c-5979-a45e-6300e8bad947" :answer/id #uuid "a76bc466-a99c-5979-a45e-6300e8bad947" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.596" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-141226aa-99b9-5761-b841-c0e97cf724ee" :answer/id #uuid "141226aa-99b9-5761-b841-c0e97cf724ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.682" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca1eb351-55b5-56cf-8dcd-fb494fd3356f" :answer/id #uuid "ca1eb351-55b5-56cf-8dcd-fb494fd3356f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.698" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a1c4477-0150-5af4-b2d4-f4066d364834" :answer/id #uuid "1a1c4477-0150-5af4-b2d4-f4066d364834" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.712" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-110563bd-a4e1-5dc9-8400-b537447d4a3c" :question/id #uuid "110563bd-a4e1-5dc9-8400-b537447d4a3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-148417" :question/problem "A company is selling a new product on three e-commerce platforms, platform $1,$ platform $2,$ and platform $3.$\n\n- $56%$ of customers bought the product on platform $1,$ and $96%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\n- $14%$ of customers bought the product on platform $2,$ and $80%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\n- $30%$ of customers bought the product on platform $3,$ and $90%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\nIf $45%$ of all customers are teenagers and $92%$ of teenage customers are satisfied with the product, what is the probability that a randomly chosen satisfied customer is a teenager? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $T$ be the event that a customer is a teenager, $S$ be the event that a customer is satisfied, and ${P}_{1},{P}_{2},{P}_{3}$ be the event that a customer bought the product on platform $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({P}_{1})=0.56 &  & P(S|{P}_{1})=0.96 \\\\  & P({P}_{2})=0.14 &  & P(S|{P}_{2})=0.8 \\\\  & P({P}_{3})=0.3 &  & P(S|{P}_{3})=0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${P}_{1}⊔{P}_{2}⊔{P}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({P}_{1})+P({P}_{2})+P({P}_{3})=0.56+0.14+0.3=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $45%$ of all customers are teenagers, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=0.45.\n$$\n\n- Since $92%$ of teenage customers are satisfied, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|T)=0.92.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen satisfied customer is a teenager, which is represented as $P(T|S).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T|S) & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S|{P}_{1})P({P}_{1})+P(S|{P}_{2})P({P}_{2})+P(S|{P}_{3})P({P}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.92)(0.45)}{(0.96)(0.56)+(0.8)(0.14)+(0.9)(0.3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.414}{0.9196} \\\\  & ≈0.450,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c73be3d6-f9cd-557f-808f-32f2584f9bbf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c73be3d6-f9cd-557f-808f-32f2584f9bbf" :answer-field/id #uuid "c73be3d6-f9cd-557f-808f-32f2584f9bbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c14f8a89-fea0-54b9-b1c5-9f5e4448898b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3c8f2023-1fee-54ee-b839-16e84ad33b05" "answer-8e350e9f-716f-532f-bd7a-2341e751c264" "answer-1ec3078b-e60d-54c6-b2c0-e39ab1d2818a" "answer-c14f8a89-fea0-54b9-b1c5-9f5e4448898b" "answer-7ba26f79-d043-5412-9c51-78634aa91c09"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c8f2023-1fee-54ee-b839-16e84ad33b05" :answer/id #uuid "3c8f2023-1fee-54ee-b839-16e84ad33b05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.124" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e350e9f-716f-532f-bd7a-2341e751c264" :answer/id #uuid "8e350e9f-716f-532f-bd7a-2341e751c264" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.355" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ec3078b-e60d-54c6-b2c0-e39ab1d2818a" :answer/id #uuid "1ec3078b-e60d-54c6-b2c0-e39ab1d2818a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.372" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c14f8a89-fea0-54b9-b1c5-9f5e4448898b" :answer/id #uuid "c14f8a89-fea0-54b9-b1c5-9f5e4448898b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.450" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ba26f79-d043-5412-9c51-78634aa91c09" :answer/id #uuid "7ba26f79-d043-5412-9c51-78634aa91c09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.485" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f7a1a1db-a551-5627-b716-a412f2910020" :question/id #uuid "f7a1a1db-a551-5627-b716-a412f2910020" :question/math-academy-id "q-149492" :question/problem "A volleyball school for children splits the players into three teams, team $1,$ team $2,$ and team $3.$\n\n- $54%$ of the children are in team $1,$ and $20%$ of players in this team have been playing volleyball for over a year.\n\n- $32%$ of the children are in team $2,$ and $30%$ of players in this team have been playing volleyball for over a year.\n\n- $14%$ of the children are in team $3,$ and $15%$ of players in this team have been playing volleyball for over a year.\n\nIf $10%$ of the children can play in all positions, and $94%$ of children that can play in all positions have been playing for over a year, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child who has been playing for over a year can play in all positions? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $B$ be the event that a child can play in all positions, $I$ be the event that a child has been playing for over a year, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a child is in team $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.54 &  & P(I|{T}_{1})=0.2 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.32 &  & P(I|{T}_{2})=0.3 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.14 &  & P(I|{T}_{3})=0.15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.54+0.32+0.15=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of children can play in all positions, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.1.\n$$\n\n- Since $94%$ of children who play in all positions have been playing for over a year, we have\n\n$$\nP(I|B)=0.94.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child who has been playing for over a year can play in all positions, which is represented as $P(B|I).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|I) & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(I|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(I|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.94)(0.1)}{(0.2)(0.54)+(0.3)(0.32)+(0.15)(0.14)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.094}{0.225} \\\\  & ≈0.418,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-da0d6d19-9eb1-5179-970a-8af07c710dc1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-da0d6d19-9eb1-5179-970a-8af07c710dc1" :answer-field/id #uuid "da0d6d19-9eb1-5179-970a-8af07c710dc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d344ed22-0120-51f9-b42a-cedaf01ced46" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b41e43a3-a236-598c-b879-c1d73ef7060d" "answer-d344ed22-0120-51f9-b42a-cedaf01ced46" "answer-5891b082-1b99-5e53-b911-542f283ae193" "answer-1b8c05fe-cf34-5642-b9f4-e1a31950c02d" "answer-092fec60-965b-5d2f-b7c9-4af5092da86b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b41e43a3-a236-598c-b879-c1d73ef7060d" :answer/id #uuid "b41e43a3-a236-598c-b879-c1d73ef7060d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.323" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d344ed22-0120-51f9-b42a-cedaf01ced46" :answer/id #uuid "d344ed22-0120-51f9-b42a-cedaf01ced46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.418" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5891b082-1b99-5e53-b911-542f283ae193" :answer/id #uuid "5891b082-1b99-5e53-b911-542f283ae193" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.482" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b8c05fe-cf34-5642-b9f4-e1a31950c02d" :answer/id #uuid "1b8c05fe-cf34-5642-b9f4-e1a31950c02d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.529" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-092fec60-965b-5d2f-b7c9-4af5092da86b" :answer/id #uuid "092fec60-965b-5d2f-b7c9-4af5092da86b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.582" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167119 :question/problem "Arthur and Sara are finding the best recipe for chocolate muffins, and they test three different oven cooking modes, mode $1,$ mode $2,$ and mode $3.$ They also try two types of yeast, A and B.\n\n- They use mode $1$ to cook the $45%$ of their muffins, and $80%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\n- They use mode $2$ to cook the $35%$ of their muffins.\n\n- They use mode $3$ to cook the $20%$ of their muffins, and $90%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\nIf $65%$ of all muffins were prepared with yeast A, $82%$ of yeast A muffins turn out well, and $68%$ of good muffins were prepared with yeast A, what is the probability that a randomly chosen muffin cooked with mode $2$ turned out well? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the event that a muffin is made with yeast A, $G$ be the event that a muffin turns out well, and ${M}_{1},{M}_{2},{M}_{3}$ be the event that a muffin is cooked with mode $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({M}_{1})=0.45 &  & P(G|{M}_{1})=0.8 \\\\  & P({M}_{2})=0.35 &  & P(G|{M}_{2}) \\\\  & P({M}_{3})=0.2 &  & P(G|{M}_{3})=0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${M}_{1}⊔{M}_{2}⊔{M}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({M}_{1})+P({M}_{2})+P({M}_{3})=0.45+0.35+0.2=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $65%$ of muffins are made with yeast A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.65.\n$$\n\n- Since $82%$ of muffins made with yeast A turned out well, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=0.82.\n$$\n\n- Since $68%$ of good muffins were prepared with yeast A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A|G)=0.68.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen muffin cooked with mode $2$ turned well, which is represented as $P(G|{M}_{2}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|G) & =\\frac{P(G|A)P(A)}{P(G)} \\\\ P(A|G) & =\\frac{P(G|A)P(A)}{P(G|{M}_{1})P({M}_{1})+P(G|{M}_{2})P({M}_{2})+P(G|{M}_{3})P({M}_{3})} \\\\ 0.68 & =\\frac{(0.82)(0.65)}{(0.8)(0.45)+P(G|{M}_{2})(0.35)+(0.9)(0.2)} \\\\ 0.68 & =\\frac{0.533}{0.35P(G|{M}_{2})+0.54}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(G|{M}_{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.68(0.35P(G|{M}_{2})+0.54) & =0.533 \\\\ 0.35P(G|{M}_{2})+0.54 & =\\frac{0.533}{0.68} \\\\ 0.35P(G|{M}_{2})+0.54 & =\\frac{533}{680} \\\\ 0.35P(G|{M}_{2}) & =\\frac{533}{680}-0.54 \\\\ P(G|{M}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{0.35}(\\frac{533}{680}-0.54) \\\\ P(G|{M}_{2}) & ≈0.697\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163792 :answer-field/correct 17592186163797 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100920 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-08f35ffe-3316-51be-b28c-866b8f0d5426"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-08f35ffe-3316-51be-b28c-866b8f0d5426" :question/id #uuid "08f35ffe-3316-51be-b28c-866b8f0d5426" :question/math-academy-id "q-166144" :question/problem "Compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))$ at $x=2.$\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=2}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))=f(x){g}^{′}(x)+{f}^{′}(x)g(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=2$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=2} & =f(2){g}^{′}(2)+{f}^{′}(2)g(2) \\\\  & =18\\cdot 4+12\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0760fbe2-793a-57f7-915e-2969c13795cf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0760fbe2-793a-57f7-915e-2969c13795cf" :answer-field/id #uuid "0760fbe2-793a-57f7-915e-2969c13795cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-fa0ea8b8-d753-5f16-ba5a-1b1d241fd8ea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fa0ea8b8-d753-5f16-ba5a-1b1d241fd8ea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa0ea8b8-d753-5f16-ba5a-1b1d241fd8ea" :answer/id #uuid "fa0ea8b8-d753-5f16-ba5a-1b1d241fd8ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "144" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105863 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c5c7d2a8-3068-5036-8a7c-19c736f09c46"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c5c7d2a8-3068-5036-8a7c-19c736f09c46" :question/id #uuid "c5c7d2a8-3068-5036-8a7c-19c736f09c46" :question/math-academy-id "q-166145" :question/problem "Find ${h}^{′}(-2)$, if $h(x)=g(f(x)).$\n\n${h}^{′}(-2)\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(f(x))\\cdot {f}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=-2$ and using the values given in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & ={g}^{′}(f(-2))\\cdot {f}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & ={g}^{′}(2)\\cdot {f}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ce65dcc6-4076-500b-9b31-e498469884f3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ce65dcc6-4076-500b-9b31-e498469884f3" :answer-field/id #uuid "ce65dcc6-4076-500b-9b31-e498469884f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a6a9515f-8460-5af2-b416-a5af4b0fb99d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a6a9515f-8460-5af2-b416-a5af4b0fb99d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6a9515f-8460-5af2-b416-a5af4b0fb99d" :answer/id #uuid "a6a9515f-8460-5af2-b416-a5af4b0fb99d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100811 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2ad31a73-80bb-53e8-b8d5-84512ffb448a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-2ad31a73-80bb-53e8-b8d5-84512ffb448a" :question/id #uuid "2ad31a73-80bb-53e8-b8d5-84512ffb448a" :question/math-academy-id "q-166146" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{x+2}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{x+2}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=-2$ directly into the limit, we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{x+2} & ={\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{x+2}|}_{x=-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3f(-2)-g(-2)}{-2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 2-6}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\frac{{(3f(x)-g(x))}^{′}}{{(x+2)}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\frac{3{f}^{′}(x)-{g}^{′}(x)}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}[3{f}^{′}(x)-{g}^{′}(x)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-2$ again, and this time, we are able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}[3{f}^{′}(x)-{g}^{′}(x)] & ={[3{f}^{′}(x)-{g}^{′}(x)]|}_{x=-2} \\\\  & =3{f}^{′}(-2)-{g}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 12-(-4) \\\\  & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{x+2}=40.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1d5bc92d-ecfb-5f57-9120-28f2a41a99a5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1d5bc92d-ecfb-5f57-9120-28f2a41a99a5" :answer-field/id #uuid "1d5bc92d-ecfb-5f57-9120-28f2a41a99a5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e1934568-267c-5dc4-b464-3347e25190ea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e1934568-267c-5dc4-b464-3347e25190ea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1934568-267c-5dc4-b464-3347e25190ea" :answer/id #uuid "e1934568-267c-5dc4-b464-3347e25190ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064160 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-caea6f22-353c-51f3-b53e-c4874e2c30a8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-caea6f22-353c-51f3-b53e-c4874e2c30a8" :question/id #uuid "caea6f22-353c-51f3-b53e-c4874e2c30a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-166147" :question/problem "![](images/7e/7e6d36041e7bd7fe1ecf7cae5f069f5714e06fb546151c7c5d1238219975e811.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=g(x)$ for $x\\in [-4,-1]$ is shown above. Complete the missing part in the statement below.\n\nThe mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-4,-1)$ such that ${g}^{′}(c)\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $g(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[-4,-1]$ and differentiable on  $(-4,-1).$\n\nGiven that $g(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following: \n\n> *There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(c)=\\frac{g(b)-g(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-4$ and $b=-1.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{g(-1)-g(-4)}{-1-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-(-1)}{-1+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n>  *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-4,-1)$ such that ${g}^{′}(c)=\\frac{2}{3}.$*" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-af16ffc8-34d4-5d9f-9c83-e6de0ef51e37"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-af16ffc8-34d4-5d9f-9c83-e6de0ef51e37" :answer-field/id #uuid "af16ffc8-34d4-5d9f-9c83-e6de0ef51e37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-61ffeebf-caf7-5a1f-9fd2-b7d28409246e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-61ffeebf-caf7-5a1f-9fd2-b7d28409246e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61ffeebf-caf7-5a1f-9fd2-b7d28409246e" :answer/id #uuid "61ffeebf-caf7-5a1f-9fd2-b7d28409246e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092390 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-93c6d227-1e4b-58a1-bf67-7c22cb760172"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-93c6d227-1e4b-58a1-bf67-7c22cb760172" :question/id #uuid "93c6d227-1e4b-58a1-bf67-7c22cb760172" :question/math-academy-id "q-166148" :question/problem "Given that $f(2.1)=21,$ approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=2.1$ using a backward difference approximation.\n\n${f}^{′}(2.1)\\,≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=2.1.$ \n\nWe choose the step size $h>0$ to be as small as possible. In this case, $h=0.1$ since the nearest backward point, for which we know the value of $f(x),$ is $f(2)=18$ (from the table above).\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2.1) & ≈\\frac{f(2.1)-f(2.1-0.1)}{0.1} \\\\  & =10(f(2.1)-f(2)) \\\\  & =10(21-18) \\\\  & =10\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-281ee34a-5ec1-50ca-8410-459bfa2da35a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-281ee34a-5ec1-50ca-8410-459bfa2da35a" :answer-field/id #uuid "281ee34a-5ec1-50ca-8410-459bfa2da35a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-df2cbeda-e03c-506c-97d7-c7b56df4f483" :answer-field/choices ["answer-df2cbeda-e03c-506c-97d7-c7b56df4f483"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df2cbeda-e03c-506c-97d7-c7b56df4f483" :answer/id #uuid "df2cbeda-e03c-506c-97d7-c7b56df4f483" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119954 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fd542e6d-453b-52cc-8c0f-6c5dc531d308"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-fd542e6d-453b-52cc-8c0f-6c5dc531d308" :question/id #uuid "fd542e6d-453b-52cc-8c0f-6c5dc531d308" :question/math-academy-id "q-166149" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=r(x),$ where $r(x)=\\frac{3}{5x-2}.$\n\nThe vertical asymptote is $x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that \n\n$$\ny=r(x)=\\frac{3}{5x-2}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n5x-2=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ is the only vertical asymptote of $y=r(x),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/61/61d7b22d6df4c7cb85a814e84a8af2f8d162fe75d25b4e3e09258bfe965719ff.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-17463037-04b2-54df-9864-31c60a5f05a8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-17463037-04b2-54df-9864-31c60a5f05a8" :answer-field/id #uuid "17463037-04b2-54df-9864-31c60a5f05a8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f5eab96d-676d-5a2c-9e29-604cf8b1f1f1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f5eab96d-676d-5a2c-9e29-604cf8b1f1f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5eab96d-676d-5a2c-9e29-604cf8b1f1f1" :answer/id #uuid "f5eab96d-676d-5a2c-9e29-604cf8b1f1f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119939 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-35035d93-2432-5072-a261-b3e11d036f56"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-35035d93-2432-5072-a261-b3e11d036f56" :question/id #uuid "35035d93-2432-5072-a261-b3e11d036f56" :question/math-academy-id "q-166151" :question/problem "Given that $x=\\frac{3}{5y-2},$ write $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ in terms of $y.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x=\\frac{3}{5y-2}$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{3}{5y-2}) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}((5y-2)^{-1}) \\\\  & =-3(5y-2)^{-2}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{(5y-2)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{15}(5y-2)^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-78ee6ce2-448a-5e5a-a2ca-c421a7bb8463"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-78ee6ce2-448a-5e5a-a2ca-c421a7bb8463" :answer-field/id #uuid "78ee6ce2-448a-5e5a-a2ca-c421a7bb8463" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-03a5abe1-8a17-5f77-aca0-0fecc6ce44b1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-03a5abe1-8a17-5f77-aca0-0fecc6ce44b1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03a5abe1-8a17-5f77-aca0-0fecc6ce44b1" :answer/id #uuid "03a5abe1-8a17-5f77-aca0-0fecc6ce44b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{(5y-2)^2}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119979 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-da95b5d5-ad91-5dbb-9815-db7c7936c826"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-da95b5d5-ad91-5dbb-9815-db7c7936c826" :question/id #uuid "da95b5d5-ad91-5dbb-9815-db7c7936c826" :question/math-academy-id "q-166152" :question/problem "Write ${({r}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${r}^{-1}(x).$\n\n$({r}^{-1})^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}} \n$\\cdot (5{r}^{-1}(x)-$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=r(x)=\\frac{3}{5x-2},$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{5y-2}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={r}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we need to differentiate with respect to $y$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$ In part 7, we found that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{15}(5y-2)^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${r}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={r}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{r}^{-1}(x)] & =-\\frac{1}{15}{(5{r}^{-1}(x)-2)}^{2} \\\\ ({r}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{15}\\cdot {(5{r}^{-1}(x)-\\,2)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-240c4af1-601a-56e4-8a24-6ce123e0c99f" "answer-field-7183c527-e212-5bc2-a3ad-d8fa7c83c6fc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-240c4af1-601a-56e4-8a24-6ce123e0c99f" :answer-field/id #uuid "240c4af1-601a-56e4-8a24-6ce123e0c99f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e4197bd9-e830-5e33-b585-2e4d2a3b0a53" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e4197bd9-e830-5e33-b585-2e4d2a3b0a53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4197bd9-e830-5e33-b585-2e4d2a3b0a53" :answer/id #uuid "e4197bd9-e830-5e33-b585-2e4d2a3b0a53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7183c527-e212-5bc2-a3ad-d8fa7c83c6fc" :answer-field/id #uuid "7183c527-e212-5bc2-a3ad-d8fa7c83c6fc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-931a5d58-e545-53ef-9d43-7a3c38508bc1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-931a5d58-e545-53ef-9d43-7a3c38508bc1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-931a5d58-e545-53ef-9d43-7a3c38508bc1" :answer/id #uuid "931a5d58-e545-53ef-9d43-7a3c38508bc1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "multistep-ff96b3c2-07b3-5d74-899d-672a49f6a4c6" :multistep/id #uuid "ff96b3c2-07b3-5d74-899d-672a49f6a4c6" :multistep/context "| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $2$ | $12$ | $6$ | $-4$ |\n| $2$ | $18$ | $12$ | $6$ | $4$ |\n\nThe functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are differentiable for all real $x.$ Some values of $f(x),$ $g(x),$ and their derivatives are given in the table above." :multistep/steps ["step-9e83fdad-015c-5732-bfdc-80d5ceb9a3ad" "step-72ff08ee-a67b-566c-9514-7420dab45774" "step-3f90975a-f188-5c18-92df-9aa069bb827f" "step-e35ab122-cd40-5393-a39b-3b0b28d306fb" "step-8e3f066e-92e5-5bf6-a13b-617247ca6557" "step-045f6731-93bd-5066-8f29-40f1ddabb374" "step-efcdb322-5868-5bbe-8cf5-87b833156dd3" "step-6a4f162e-5f2f-506b-b3ad-81b4b2420a5f"] :multistep/first-step "step-9e83fdad-015c-5732-bfdc-80d5ceb9a3ad" :db/ensure [:multistep/validate]} {:db/id "step-9e83fdad-015c-5732-bfdc-80d5ceb9a3ad" :step/id #uuid "9e83fdad-015c-5732-bfdc-80d5ceb9a3ad" :step/content "question-08f35ffe-3316-51be-b28c-866b8f0d5426" :step/next "step-72ff08ee-a67b-566c-9514-7420dab45774" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-72ff08ee-a67b-566c-9514-7420dab45774" :step/id #uuid "72ff08ee-a67b-566c-9514-7420dab45774" :step/content "question-c5c7d2a8-3068-5036-8a7c-19c736f09c46" :step/next "step-3f90975a-f188-5c18-92df-9aa069bb827f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3f90975a-f188-5c18-92df-9aa069bb827f" :step/id #uuid "3f90975a-f188-5c18-92df-9aa069bb827f" :step/content "question-2ad31a73-80bb-53e8-b8d5-84512ffb448a" :step/next "step-e35ab122-cd40-5393-a39b-3b0b28d306fb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e35ab122-cd40-5393-a39b-3b0b28d306fb" :step/id #uuid "e35ab122-cd40-5393-a39b-3b0b28d306fb" :step/content "question-caea6f22-353c-51f3-b53e-c4874e2c30a8" :step/next "step-8e3f066e-92e5-5bf6-a13b-617247ca6557" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8e3f066e-92e5-5bf6-a13b-617247ca6557" :step/id #uuid "8e3f066e-92e5-5bf6-a13b-617247ca6557" :step/content "question-93c6d227-1e4b-58a1-bf67-7c22cb760172" :step/next "step-045f6731-93bd-5066-8f29-40f1ddabb374" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-045f6731-93bd-5066-8f29-40f1ddabb374" :step/id #uuid "045f6731-93bd-5066-8f29-40f1ddabb374" :step/content "question-fd542e6d-453b-52cc-8c0f-6c5dc531d308" :step/next "step-efcdb322-5868-5bbe-8cf5-87b833156dd3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-efcdb322-5868-5bbe-8cf5-87b833156dd3" :step/id #uuid "efcdb322-5868-5bbe-8cf5-87b833156dd3" :step/content "question-35035d93-2432-5072-a261-b3e11d036f56" :step/next "step-6a4f162e-5f2f-506b-b3ad-81b4b2420a5f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6a4f162e-5f2f-506b-b3ad-81b4b2420a5f" :step/id #uuid "6a4f162e-5f2f-506b-b3ad-81b4b2420a5f" :step/content "question-da95b5d5-ad91-5dbb-9815-db7c7936c826" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "assigned-problem-2757a9cb-7ab6-5d75-9840-e43ae83a6b15" :assigned-problem/id #uuid "2757a9cb-7ab6-5d75-9840-e43ae83a6b15" :assigned-problem/content "multistep-ff96b3c2-07b3-5d74-899d-672a49f6a4c6" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [17592186050961 17592186050400 17592186050821 17592186048434 17592186049236 17592186048163 17592186049248 17592186048776] :db/ensure [:assigned-problem/validate]} {:db/id "activity-a46c1bc8-8f1e-5c4c-8602-ea418f79eceb" :activity/id #uuid "a46c1bc8-8f1e-5c4c-8602-ea418f79eceb" :activity/title "Applying the Chain Rule and L'Hopital's Rule to Data Tables" :activity/type :activity.type/assignment :activity/steps ["step-a3aff545-1bb2-5fdf-8a33-aed0c8fa3ac5"] :activity/first-step "step-a3aff545-1bb2-5fdf-8a33-aed0c8fa3ac5" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-a3aff545-1bb2-5fdf-8a33-aed0c8fa3ac5" :step/id #uuid "a3aff545-1bb2-5fdf-8a33-aed0c8fa3ac5" :step/content "assigned-problem-2757a9cb-7ab6-5d75-9840-e43ae83a6b15" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188275 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1706fef5-2c32-54fe-bdcf-eb9228266f86"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188535 :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3y, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 0.5,Y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot 3y \\\\  & =6xy,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [1,\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6xy, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 0.5,Y\\le 1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2/3}}6xy\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}6x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2/3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{0.5} \\\\  & =(0.5)^{2}-0 \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186185490 :answer-field/correct 17592186185493 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188561 :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}y, & \\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(0\\le X\\le 0.4,Y\\le 2).$ Round your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}y \\\\  & =y\\sqrt{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [1,2].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 0.4,Y\\le 2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}\\sqrt{x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{0.4} \\\\  & =(0.4)^{3/2}-0 \\\\  & ≈0.253.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186185646 :answer-field/correct 17592186185650 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-1706fef5-2c32-54fe-bdcf-eb9228266f86" :question/id #uuid "1706fef5-2c32-54fe-bdcf-eb9228266f86" :question/math-academy-id "q-143364" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}, & \\,0\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}y, & \\,3\\le y\\le 5, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\ge 2,3\\le Y\\le 4).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}y{e}^{-x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,\\infty )\\times [3,5].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}y{e}^{-x}, & \\,0\\le x,\\,3\\le y\\le 5 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2,3\\le Y\\le 4) & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{8}y{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{-x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{3}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{16}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{b}\\frac{7}{16}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{7{e}^{-x}}{16}]}_{2}^{b} \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{7{e}^{-b}}{16}+\\frac{7{e}^{-2}}{16}] \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{16{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1b00f6e3-8cc3-5080-b638-0c0a437c40d6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1b00f6e3-8cc3-5080-b638-0c0a437c40d6" :answer-field/id #uuid "1b00f6e3-8cc3-5080-b638-0c0a437c40d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8f6c7f06-285b-5716-8654-ada585b5cad3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6ca1210b-5ee2-5865-924d-eb975f41a198" "answer-e274eb34-e730-5088-9d44-3c0f08b82dba" "answer-8f6c7f06-285b-5716-8654-ada585b5cad3" "answer-22b3a3fc-340b-5ed5-8d5e-990986db2466" "answer-411a9cec-3a8e-59f5-9ad3-e086842f889a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ca1210b-5ee2-5865-924d-eb975f41a198" :answer/id #uuid "6ca1210b-5ee2-5865-924d-eb975f41a198" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e274eb34-e730-5088-9d44-3c0f08b82dba" :answer/id #uuid "e274eb34-e730-5088-9d44-3c0f08b82dba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16{e}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f6c7f06-285b-5716-8654-ada585b5cad3" :answer/id #uuid "8f6c7f06-285b-5716-8654-ada585b5cad3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{16{e}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22b3a3fc-340b-5ed5-8d5e-990986db2466" :answer/id #uuid "22b3a3fc-340b-5ed5-8d5e-990986db2466" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-411a9cec-3a8e-59f5-9ad3-e086842f889a" :answer/id #uuid "411a9cec-3a8e-59f5-9ad3-e086842f889a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8{e}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188295 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-02ed11c8-435c-5d94-883f-47ba8fa464eb" "question-09b415e6-d89a-5c4c-91d7-faeef6a95b62"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-02ed11c8-435c-5d94-883f-47ba8fa464eb" :question/id #uuid "02ed11c8-435c-5d94-883f-47ba8fa464eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-143377" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{2}{3}y$ for $y\\in [1,2]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}y{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [1,2].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}(2-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [1,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(4{x}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{2}{3}y)}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-54963578-cc23-5bef-b243-20d224d918a2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-54963578-cc23-5bef-b243-20d224d918a2" :answer-field/id #uuid "54963578-cc23-5bef-b243-20d224d918a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-94c5f700-7024-5ad9-9fb8-06de616c6958" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fbb5fdb8-bd3a-507b-bfd0-0f58c8d15589" "answer-8a10c141-494b-5ffe-b789-4d235572d460" "answer-94c5f700-7024-5ad9-9fb8-06de616c6958" "answer-a350e58a-9fe9-5a48-a78c-f6ab313c004a" "answer-f085a1c6-cfcd-5341-bcfa-3848ba092d59"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbb5fdb8-bd3a-507b-bfd0-0f58c8d15589" :answer/id #uuid "fbb5fdb8-bd3a-507b-bfd0-0f58c8d15589" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a10c141-494b-5ffe-b789-4d235572d460" :answer/id #uuid "8a10c141-494b-5ffe-b789-4d235572d460" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94c5f700-7024-5ad9-9fb8-06de616c6958" :answer/id #uuid "94c5f700-7024-5ad9-9fb8-06de616c6958" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a350e58a-9fe9-5a48-a78c-f6ab313c004a" :answer/id #uuid "a350e58a-9fe9-5a48-a78c-f6ab313c004a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f085a1c6-cfcd-5341-bcfa-3848ba092d59" :answer/id #uuid "f085a1c6-cfcd-5341-bcfa-3848ba092d59" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-09b415e6-d89a-5c4c-91d7-faeef6a95b62" :question/id #uuid "09b415e6-d89a-5c4c-91d7-faeef6a95b62" :question/math-academy-id "q-143387" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}(x+y),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{1}{4}(y+\\frac{3}{2})$ for $y\\in [2,3]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+yx]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2+2y-\\frac{1}{2}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(y+\\frac{3}{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [2,3].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=2}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+\\frac{9}{2}-2x-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+\\frac{5}{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [1,2].$ So, when $x\\in [1,2]$ and $y\\in [2,3],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{1}{4}(x+y)}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}\\ne {{\\frac{1}{4}(x+\\frac{5}{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{\\frac{1}{4}(y+\\frac{3}{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is *not* true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9e41b8cd-f547-56b5-b624-9d4998a39501"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9e41b8cd-f547-56b5-b624-9d4998a39501" :answer-field/id #uuid "9e41b8cd-f547-56b5-b624-9d4998a39501" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-afa9fea7-5a60-5e00-be15-b60751c4ec57" :answer-field/choices ["answer-310a997c-e1ee-509f-b5a5-c83f303016e8" "answer-afa9fea7-5a60-5e00-be15-b60751c4ec57" "answer-f0b9033f-aefa-5a72-bc8f-61f04664c8aa" "answer-96855423-2c14-53c8-9841-2610cea6223a" "answer-e2d3546d-16f4-553d-bc58-12b528100c36"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-310a997c-e1ee-509f-b5a5-c83f303016e8" :answer/id #uuid "310a997c-e1ee-509f-b5a5-c83f303016e8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-afa9fea7-5a60-5e00-be15-b60751c4ec57" :answer/id #uuid "afa9fea7-5a60-5e00-be15-b60751c4ec57" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0b9033f-aefa-5a72-bc8f-61f04664c8aa" :answer/id #uuid "f0b9033f-aefa-5a72-bc8f-61f04664c8aa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96855423-2c14-53c8-9841-2610cea6223a" :answer/id #uuid "96855423-2c14-53c8-9841-2610cea6223a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2d3546d-16f4-553d-bc58-12b528100c36" :answer/id #uuid "e2d3546d-16f4-553d-bc58-12b528100c36" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-0a3d1120-832a-5700-ba94-05d6bb26c8cd" :activity/id #uuid "0a3d1120-832a-5700-ba94-05d6bb26c8cd" :activity/title "Independence of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050682 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-f08653cb-bc58-5a7a-a441-4c86bbb7eea7" "step-829bd28e-43ba-5401-9217-1577c3b42966" "step-4cb124b6-2f91-5b80-98ad-73768b8d6164" "step-3d7ed4a8-d1ca-5534-8d7d-2245f866ff37" "step-6e0cb841-b7e4-52d1-9070-be7f75eefde3"] :activity/first-step "step-f08653cb-bc58-5a7a-a441-4c86bbb7eea7" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-f08653cb-bc58-5a7a-a441-4c86bbb7eea7" :step/id #uuid "f08653cb-bc58-5a7a-a441-4c86bbb7eea7" :step/content 17592186188535 :step/next "step-829bd28e-43ba-5401-9217-1577c3b42966" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-829bd28e-43ba-5401-9217-1577c3b42966" :step/id #uuid "829bd28e-43ba-5401-9217-1577c3b42966" :step/content 17592186188561 :step/next "step-4cb124b6-2f91-5b80-98ad-73768b8d6164" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4cb124b6-2f91-5b80-98ad-73768b8d6164" :step/id #uuid "4cb124b6-2f91-5b80-98ad-73768b8d6164" :step/content "question-1706fef5-2c32-54fe-bdcf-eb9228266f86" :step/next "step-3d7ed4a8-d1ca-5534-8d7d-2245f866ff37" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3d7ed4a8-d1ca-5534-8d7d-2245f866ff37" :step/id #uuid "3d7ed4a8-d1ca-5534-8d7d-2245f866ff37" :step/content "question-02ed11c8-435c-5d94-883f-47ba8fa464eb" :step/next "step-6e0cb841-b7e4-52d1-9070-be7f75eefde3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6e0cb841-b7e4-52d1-9070-be7f75eefde3" :step/id #uuid "6e0cb841-b7e4-52d1-9070-be7f75eefde3" :step/content "question-09b415e6-d89a-5c4c-91d7-faeef6a95b62" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115259 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f10d9718-f09b-5a57-a9a5-0bd402337019"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115088 :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=f(x+2)$ is a horizontal translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2$ units to the *left*. So, every point on the graph of $y=f(x+2)$ is two units *to the left* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/75/758899a1abd5298429059016a4127db61eda014e8c46e0539e9226c5641e5ceb.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115260 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9f46143b-fdb8-50df-bf49-6caa0060fc7f" "question-39cdb9cb-c996-510c-9952-40829263e555"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115089 :question/problem "The point $P$ with coordinates $(-2,0)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)$. Find a point that must lie on $y=f(x-7).$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=f(x-7)$ is a translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $7$ units to the *right*. So the $y$-coordinate remains unchanged while the $x$-coordinate increases by $7.$\n\nWe know that the point $(-2,0)$ lies on $y=f(x).$ Therefore, the point $(-2+7,0) = (5,0)$ must lie on $y=f(x-7).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115261 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c3371da1-431a-54c0-b96b-91167f5060d5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115090 :question/worked-solution "To graph $y=f(x+1)-2,$ we translate the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $1$ unit to the left and then $2$ units down.\n\n![](images/17/1744139728266d53c546e0b98452e042b8d099cebed12f5ee0a7c230257e9fa2.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115274 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-44298347-9f79-536a-ae21-5b66403bb6c0" "question-8c7a6d9e-5cbe-57ca-9331-32f4ca36decb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115103 :question/problem "The point $P$ with coordinates $(1, 0)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x) .$ What point must lie on $y=f(x + 1)+5?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=f(x+1)+5$  is a translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $1$ unit to the *left* and $5$ units up. So the $y$-coordinate increases by $5$ and the $x$-coordinate decreases by $1.$\n\nWe know that the point $P(1,0)$ lies on $y=f(x).$ Therefore, the point $(1-1,0+5) = (0,5)$ must lie on $y=f(x+1)+5.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-f10d9718-f09b-5a57-a9a5-0bd402337019" :question/id #uuid "f10d9718-f09b-5a57-a9a5-0bd402337019" :question/math-academy-id "q-34672" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b8a944368cb4549780c5868c164a57d55fffff205ef0816b7316cb9fcf5a93dc.png)\n\nGiven the curve $y=f(x)$ and the curve $y=f(x+a)$, which of the following is the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "Every point on the graph of $y=f(x+a)$ is $2$ units *to the left* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x).$ So the graph of $y=f(x+a)$ is a horizontal translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2$ units to the *left*. \n\nConsequently, $y=f(x+a)=f(x+2)$ and so $a=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5f260f2b-acd0-58c2-b56d-1f9b044c4797"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5f260f2b-acd0-58c2-b56d-1f9b044c4797" :answer-field/id #uuid "5f260f2b-acd0-58c2-b56d-1f9b044c4797" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d3bb863f-e804-568f-9536-a01cbc1d024f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d5b7b5b3-7025-5803-b5cd-7ffc7342a5df" "answer-dae7ff61-54fc-5b6a-a0ec-a0048b5f802f" "answer-767ef9f6-4ec4-5a15-aa5e-723ded6627db" "answer-04ea9c31-066d-5652-a164-4fc1fda7f5d3" "answer-d3bb863f-e804-568f-9536-a01cbc1d024f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5b7b5b3-7025-5803-b5cd-7ffc7342a5df" :answer/id #uuid "d5b7b5b3-7025-5803-b5cd-7ffc7342a5df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dae7ff61-54fc-5b6a-a0ec-a0048b5f802f" :answer/id #uuid "dae7ff61-54fc-5b6a-a0ec-a0048b5f802f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-767ef9f6-4ec4-5a15-aa5e-723ded6627db" :answer/id #uuid "767ef9f6-4ec4-5a15-aa5e-723ded6627db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04ea9c31-066d-5652-a164-4fc1fda7f5d3" :answer/id #uuid "04ea9c31-066d-5652-a164-4fc1fda7f5d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3bb863f-e804-568f-9536-a01cbc1d024f" :answer/id #uuid "d3bb863f-e804-568f-9536-a01cbc1d024f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115506 :question/problem "![](images/55/5573133cd1a199d53254947f951999a10731d980faffcf75e25ac6c3e66cd861.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=f(x-1)$ is a horizontal translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $1$ unit to the *right*. So, every point on the graph of $y=f(x-1)$ is one unit *to the right* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/96/96e78d86fc113988887c0c35daf2ebf119f2f47be8d82822d439b2f47433c31d.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186112045 :answer-field/correct 17592186112046 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-9f46143b-fdb8-50df-bf49-6caa0060fc7f" :question/id #uuid "9f46143b-fdb8-50df-bf49-6caa0060fc7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-70399" :question/problem "The point $P$ with coordinates $(1,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(x-4)?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=f(x-4)$ is a translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $4$ units to the *right*. So the $y$-coordinate remains unchanged while the $x$-coordinate increases by $4.$\n\nWe know that the point $(1,1)$ lies on $y=f(x).$ Therefore, the point $(1+4,1)=(5,1)$ must lie on $y=f(x-4).$\n\n![](images/8c/8c736b4ad424cfc7efe3cd7218f873655b5bface8fe4cc670b8185ff1b73f5e5.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5fcce4a0-897e-5fde-9536-5a4f0e1e870a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5fcce4a0-897e-5fde-9536-5a4f0e1e870a" :answer-field/id #uuid "5fcce4a0-897e-5fde-9536-5a4f0e1e870a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6cc81242-fc50-5712-ad13-ddb7870716c6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d35fe771-29a2-5a54-986e-d9947dff2727" "answer-af3fc2ee-4622-517a-a981-1ef92ebc4f62" "answer-3dcad000-3cc4-5bb3-96d9-ce67b820e4b6" "answer-bc52afa7-72d6-50aa-8279-a45af0dd9f53" "answer-6cc81242-fc50-5712-ad13-ddb7870716c6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d35fe771-29a2-5a54-986e-d9947dff2727" :answer/id #uuid "d35fe771-29a2-5a54-986e-d9947dff2727" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af3fc2ee-4622-517a-a981-1ef92ebc4f62" :answer/id #uuid "af3fc2ee-4622-517a-a981-1ef92ebc4f62" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3dcad000-3cc4-5bb3-96d9-ce67b820e4b6" :answer/id #uuid "3dcad000-3cc4-5bb3-96d9-ce67b820e4b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc52afa7-72d6-50aa-8279-a45af0dd9f53" :answer/id #uuid "bc52afa7-72d6-50aa-8279-a45af0dd9f53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6cc81242-fc50-5712-ad13-ddb7870716c6" :answer/id #uuid "6cc81242-fc50-5712-ad13-ddb7870716c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(5,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-39cdb9cb-c996-510c-9952-40829263e555" :question/id #uuid "39cdb9cb-c996-510c-9952-40829263e555" :question/math-academy-id "q-70398" :question/problem "The point $P$ with coordinates $(1,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(x+4)?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=f(x+4)$ is a translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $4$ units to the *left*. So the $y$-coordinate remains unchanged while the $x$-coordinate decreases by $4.$\n\nWe know that the point $(1,3)$ lies on $y=f(x).$ Therefore, the point $(1-4,3)=(-3,3)$ must lie on $y=f(x+4).$\n\n![](images/2a/2a7333b3778aed3b4a9d744c6a100577fdeeafc497ac9d7f33670908163551b2.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-33d2b5fb-4d11-5cae-853f-e747c0eae138"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-33d2b5fb-4d11-5cae-853f-e747c0eae138" :answer-field/id #uuid "33d2b5fb-4d11-5cae-853f-e747c0eae138" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-42ed0312-6a97-5afe-81c4-371cc07a5a7f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c09ee531-8050-5ebc-a580-9f1799c0b3fa" "answer-42ed0312-6a97-5afe-81c4-371cc07a5a7f" "answer-0d64ca17-c947-58ed-8484-2b4faddc327b" "answer-3c4095b4-65a1-56f2-ac3d-93ac60413253" "answer-62243a18-d6eb-5824-aa8f-0549aac6858a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c09ee531-8050-5ebc-a580-9f1799c0b3fa" :answer/id #uuid "c09ee531-8050-5ebc-a580-9f1799c0b3fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42ed0312-6a97-5afe-81c4-371cc07a5a7f" :answer/id #uuid "42ed0312-6a97-5afe-81c4-371cc07a5a7f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d64ca17-c947-58ed-8484-2b4faddc327b" :answer/id #uuid "0d64ca17-c947-58ed-8484-2b4faddc327b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c4095b4-65a1-56f2-ac3d-93ac60413253" :answer/id #uuid "3c4095b4-65a1-56f2-ac3d-93ac60413253" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,7)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62243a18-d6eb-5824-aa8f-0549aac6858a" :answer/id #uuid "62243a18-d6eb-5824-aa8f-0549aac6858a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(5,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115508 :question/problem "![](images/f3/f35440c0132f7e98617f2d700b60563d44b7e9cb3af3850f557d4b169cc08bb7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x-3)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "To graph $y=f(x-3)+2,$ we translate the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $3$ units to the right and then $2$ units up.\n\n![](images/3c/3cdbb6ef28bc47734820fadaef1e65061775b8c96724713f7f8de30e8a5d0d5e.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186112057 :answer-field/correct 17592186112058 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-c3371da1-431a-54c0-b96b-91167f5060d5" :question/id #uuid "c3371da1-431a-54c0-b96b-91167f5060d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-110147" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7b89a8737e3fcb062d5cb53a8832bc3d8f30273696b3be0e8c47d25ba158fa60.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$, where $g(x)=f(x+a)+b.$ What are the values of $a$ and $b?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that every point on the graph $y=g(x)$ is three units *to the left* and two units *up* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x).$ \n\nSo, we conclude that $g(x)=f(x+3)+2,$ and therefore $a=3$ and $b=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-662d422b-1530-5ba0-98a4-0880cce3feef"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-662d422b-1530-5ba0-98a4-0880cce3feef" :answer-field/id #uuid "662d422b-1530-5ba0-98a4-0880cce3feef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ed238e4d-c38f-5aeb-a9d0-826a5dda4697" :answer-field/choices ["answer-981db8c4-da69-5c83-845c-0f8f09f2acaa" "answer-e1ee2e54-ef77-58b9-851d-13d05f34589d" "answer-4faa0bb8-8326-5058-8667-97b855fd0f03" "answer-20b47aad-7f2a-5a97-9da1-711cbe595051" "answer-ed238e4d-c38f-5aeb-a9d0-826a5dda4697"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-981db8c4-da69-5c83-845c-0f8f09f2acaa" :answer/id #uuid "981db8c4-da69-5c83-845c-0f8f09f2acaa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=-2,\\,b=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1ee2e54-ef77-58b9-851d-13d05f34589d" :answer/id #uuid "e1ee2e54-ef77-58b9-851d-13d05f34589d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=-3,\\,b=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4faa0bb8-8326-5058-8667-97b855fd0f03" :answer/id #uuid "4faa0bb8-8326-5058-8667-97b855fd0f03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=2,\\,b=-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20b47aad-7f2a-5a97-9da1-711cbe595051" :answer/id #uuid "20b47aad-7f2a-5a97-9da1-711cbe595051" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=3,\\,b=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed238e4d-c38f-5aeb-a9d0-826a5dda4697" :answer/id #uuid "ed238e4d-c38f-5aeb-a9d0-826a5dda4697" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=3,\\,b=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-44298347-9f79-536a-ae21-5b66403bb6c0" :question/id #uuid "44298347-9f79-536a-ae21-5b66403bb6c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-110268" :question/problem "The point $P$ with coordinates $(1,2)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(x+5)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=f(x+5)+2$  is a translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $5$ units to the *left* and $2$ units up. So the $y$-coordinate increases by $2$ and the $x$-coordinate decreases by $5.$\n\nWe know that the point $(1,2)$ lies on $y=f(x).$ Therefore, the point $(1-5,2+2)=(-4,4)$ must lie on $y=f(x+5)+2.$\n\n![](images/00/00557550846a020f437c6187dfe237b4e1cb874eca2bb1cf2bd70416e0e8d97c.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9776b31f-8640-5e03-aee4-a9445951e6de"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9776b31f-8640-5e03-aee4-a9445951e6de" :answer-field/id #uuid "9776b31f-8640-5e03-aee4-a9445951e6de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-253750c5-fb5b-5091-b589-190f83fde901" :answer-field/choices ["answer-343a6236-e67e-511d-86a7-fcbebbd3f0c7" "answer-528da259-0eed-5175-be85-38acc3e05272" "answer-253750c5-fb5b-5091-b589-190f83fde901" "answer-d3cda7fd-69e3-5b1a-abe8-7a7edabb5729" "answer-83f2e323-c3d8-5e6f-a0d0-0868dd0ebd6f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-343a6236-e67e-511d-86a7-fcbebbd3f0c7" :answer/id #uuid "343a6236-e67e-511d-86a7-fcbebbd3f0c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,7)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-528da259-0eed-5175-be85-38acc3e05272" :answer/id #uuid "528da259-0eed-5175-be85-38acc3e05272" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-4,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-253750c5-fb5b-5091-b589-190f83fde901" :answer/id #uuid "253750c5-fb5b-5091-b589-190f83fde901" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-4,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3cda7fd-69e3-5b1a-abe8-7a7edabb5729" :answer/id #uuid "d3cda7fd-69e3-5b1a-abe8-7a7edabb5729" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,7)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83f2e323-c3d8-5e6f-a0d0-0868dd0ebd6f" :answer/id #uuid "83f2e323-c3d8-5e6f-a0d0-0868dd0ebd6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(6,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8c7a6d9e-5cbe-57ca-9331-32f4ca36decb" :question/id #uuid "8c7a6d9e-5cbe-57ca-9331-32f4ca36decb" :question/math-academy-id "q-72294" :question/problem "The point $P$ with coordinates $(0,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(x-1)-2?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=f(x-1)-2$ is a translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $1$ unit to the *right* and $2$ units *down*. So the $y$-coordinate decreases by $2$ and the $x$-coordinate increases by $1.$\n\nWe know that the point $(0,3)$ lies on $y=f(x).$ Therefore, the point $(0+1,3-2)=(1,1)$ must lie on $y=f(x-1)-2.$\n\n![](images/64/64d0dc944682b12702eb8a2fa9de0ef3e65cc57ac1cc7a242a09c986ca7f955c.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1bdfdaa2-a65e-5470-970a-f7ae1c96194c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1bdfdaa2-a65e-5470-970a-f7ae1c96194c" :answer-field/id #uuid "1bdfdaa2-a65e-5470-970a-f7ae1c96194c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-91825f07-3800-5d91-9a87-771d92a21e89" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c7608469-92f8-5e24-9662-94400215b327" "answer-c2ed0611-cdfb-5d8f-8208-a35a23108ed1" "answer-15fa8f48-3b9f-5a19-861a-ca8945c6f29f" "answer-91825f07-3800-5d91-9a87-771d92a21e89" "answer-7d07ecb0-0d8f-5957-8454-8750d51c7413"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7608469-92f8-5e24-9662-94400215b327" :answer/id #uuid "c7608469-92f8-5e24-9662-94400215b327" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2ed0611-cdfb-5d8f-8208-a35a23108ed1" :answer/id #uuid "c2ed0611-cdfb-5d8f-8208-a35a23108ed1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15fa8f48-3b9f-5a19-861a-ca8945c6f29f" :answer/id #uuid "15fa8f48-3b9f-5a19-861a-ca8945c6f29f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91825f07-3800-5d91-9a87-771d92a21e89" :answer/id #uuid "91825f07-3800-5d91-9a87-771d92a21e89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d07ecb0-0d8f-5957-8454-8750d51c7413" :answer/id #uuid "7d07ecb0-0d8f-5957-8454-8750d51c7413" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116503 :tutorial/content "In general, for $k>0,$\n\n- the graph of $y=f(x-k)$ is a horizontal translation of $y=f(x)$ by $k$ units to the *right*, while\n\n- the graph of $y=f(x+k)$ is a horizontal translation of $y=f(x)$ by $k$ units to the *left*.\n\n**Watch out!** It may seem counterintuitive that subtracting $k$ moves the graph to the right, while adding $k$ moves the graph to the left. However, this is in fact true.\n\nTo see why, let's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below. Let's use this graph to plot the graph of $y=f(x-2).$\n\n![](images/6c/6ca96bba855899f9b87ae06c166503c9af739fcd6bb2ec466a73b75cc70dde7f.png)\n\nLet's take some points on the graph of $y=f(x)$ and find the corresponding points on the graphs of $y=f(x-2).$ \n\nFor example, take the point $(3,-4).$ The fact that it's on the graph of $y=f(x)$ means that $f(3)=-4.$ Then if $x={\\color{red}5},$ we have \n\n$$\nf(x-2) = f({\\color{red}5}-2) = f(3) = -4,\n$$\n\n \nwhich means that $({\\color{red}5},-4)$ is on the graph of $y=f(x-2).$ \n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$ with the same $y$-value and a $x$-value that is $2$ units *greater*! Several more examples of points are shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math]} & \\text{Points on [math]y=f(x-2)[/math]} \\\\ (3,-4) & (5,-4) \\\\ (1,2) & (3,2) \\\\ (0,1.5) & (2,1.5) \\\\ (-2,0) & (0,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's plot both curves.\n\n![](images/ed/edae088984432dc7d0fff816a9e016ad221a3c897a78cf79efe6c784fa9176fe.png)\n\nWe see that every point on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$ is shifted $2$ units *to the right* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x).$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115418 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
